ALG—antilymphocyte globulin ANS—autonomic nervous system ATPase—adenosine triphosphatase AUC—area under the (blood concentration vs. time) curve AV—atrioventricular","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"V—arteriovenous"},{"label":"B","text":"fragilis—Bacteroides fragilis BAL—British anti-Lewisite (dimercaprol) BPH—benign prostatic hypertrophy BPM—beats per minute BUN—blood urea nitrogen Cav—average (mean) plasma concentration Cmax—maximum plasma concentration Cmin—minimum plasma concentration Css—steady-state plasma concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"albicans—Candida albicans"},{"label":"C","text":"botulinum—Clostridium botulinum"},{"label":"C","text":"difficile—Clostridium difficile"},{"label":"C","text":"neoformans—Cryptococcus neoformans CAD—coronary artery disease CCB—calcium channel blocker xxiii Copyright © 2007 by The McGraw-Hill Companies, Inc. Click here for terms of use. xxiv List of Abbreviations and Acronyms CHD—coronary heart disease CHF—congestive heart failure CK—creatine kinase Cl—clearance (of drug) Cl−—chloride Cltotal — total body clearance CNS—central nervous system COMT—catechol-O-methyltransferase COPD—chronic obstructive pulmonary disease COX—cyclooxygenase(s); may be modified as COX-1 or COX-2 CRF—corticotropin-releasing factor CSF—cerebrospinal fluid CYP—cytochrome P450 (system or member of it) D1 or D2—dopamine D1 or D2 receptor DA—dopamine DHT—dihydrotestosterone DOPA—dihydroxyphenylalanine DVT—deep venous thrombosis ECG—electrocardiogram; EKG"},{"label":"E","text":"coli—Escherichia coli EDRF—endothelium-derived relaxing factor (nitric oxide) EEG—electroencephalogram EKG—electrocardiogram EPI—epinephrine ER—endoplasmic reticulum EtOH—ethanol FH2—7,8-dihydrofolic acid FH4—5,6,7,8-tetrahydrofolic acid FSH—follicle-stimulating hormone FU—fluorouracil"},{"label":"G","text":"lamblia—Giardia lamblia G protein—guanine nucleotide-binding protein GABA—γ-aminobutyric acid"},{"label":"G","text":"CSF—granulocyte colony–stimulating factor GERD—gastroesophageal reflux disease GI—gastrointestinal GM-CSF—granulocyte macrophage colony–stimulating factor GnRH—gonadotropin-releasing hormone GSH, GSSG—glutathione, reduced or oxidized GU—genitourinary H1—histamine H1 receptor H2—histamine H2 receptor"},{"label":"H","text":"influenzae—Haemophilus influenzae"},{"label":"H","text":"pylori—Helicobacter pylori Hb—hemoglobin List of Abbreviations and Acronyms xxv hCG—human chorionic gonadotropin HF—heart failure HDL—high-density lipoprotein HIT—heparin-induced thrombocytopenia HIV—human immunodeficiency virus H+, K+, ATPase—hydrogen–potassium–adenosine triphosphatase; proton pump hMG—human menopausal gonadotropin HMG—CoA-β-hydroxy-β-methylglutaryl-coenzyme A HRT—hormone replacement therapy HTN—hypertension IDDM—insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus, often called (not always appropriately) Type I diabetes mellitus IgE, G (etc.)—immunoglobulin E, G, etc. IL (-1, -2, etc.)—interleukin(s)-1, -2, etc. IM—intramuscular(ly) INH—isoniazid IP3—inositol-1,4,5-trisphosphate IV—intravenous(ly) ke—elimination rate constant K. pneumoniae—Klebsiella pneumoniae L. pneumophilia—Legionella pneumophilia L-dopa—levodopa L-thyroxine (T4)—levothyroxine LDL—low-density lipoprotein LHRH—luteinizing hormone–releasing hormone (hypothalamic) LSD—lysergic acid diethylamide LT—leukotriene MAO—monoamine oxidase MAO-A, -B—MAO type A, type B MAOI—monoamine oxidase inhibitor MI—myocardial infarction mRNA—messenger ribonucleic acid MTX—methotrexate NE—norepinephrine NM receptors—nicotinic-skeletal muscle receptors (found at the skeletal-somatic neuromuscular junction) NN receptors—nicotinic-neural receptors (found in sympathetic ganglia and on cells of the adrenal (suprarenal) medulla) N. gonorrhoeae—Neisseria gonorrhoeae NADH—nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide NADPH—nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate Na+, K+, ATPase—sodium–potassium–adenosine triphosphatase NAPA—N-acetylprocainamide NE—norepinephrine NIDDM—non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus; usually associated with Type II diabetes mellitus xxvi List of Abbreviations and Acronyms NMDA—N-methyl-D-aspartate (glutamate channel) NMS—neuroleptic malignant syndrome NNRTI—nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor NPH—isophane (Neutral protamine Hagedorn) insulin NRTI—nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor NSAID—nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (nonopioid analgesic/antipyretic) NTG—nitroglycerin P450—the cytochrome P450 mixed-function oxidase system P. aeruginosa—Pseudomonas aeruginosa P. carinii—Pneumocystis carinii P. falciparum—Plasmodium falciparum P. mirabilis—Proteus mirabilis P. vivax—Plasmodium vivax PABA—p-aminobenzoic acid PAC—premature atrial contraction PAM (2-PAM)—pralidoxime PAS—para-aminosalicylic acid PDGF—platelet-derived growth factor PG —prostaglandin PGE1—prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil) PGE2—prostaglandin E2 (dinoprostone) PGI2—prostaglandin I2 (prostacyclin) PNS—parasympathetic nervous system PO—(administration route) by mouth (per os) PO2—partial pressure (tension) of oxygen, arterial PPD—purified protein derivative of tuberculin protein G—guanine nucleotide–binding protein PTH—parathyroid hormone PVC—premature ventricular contraction RDA—recommended daily allowance REM—rapid eye movement"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","source_attribution_in_option","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8d6b05452d59","stem":"Azithromycin, an antibiotic, has an apparent volume of distribution (Vd) of approximately 30 L/kg. The best interpretation of this information is that azithromycin is which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Effective only when given intravenously"},{"label":"B","text":"Eliminated mainly by renal excretion, without prior metabolism"},{"label":"C","text":"Extensively distributed to sites outside the vascular and interstitial spaces"},{"label":"D","text":"Not extensively bound to plasma proteins"},{"label":"E","text":"Unable to cross the blood-brain or placental barriers"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-befb80dd75da","stem":"Experimental evaluation of the pharmacokinetics of a drug under development leads to the finding that it “undergoes significant first-pass hepatic metabolism.” Which of the following administration routes was most likely used to reach this conclusion?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intramuscular"},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous"},{"label":"C","text":"Oral"},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal"},{"label":"E","text":"Sublingual (SL) 1 Copyright © 2007 by The McGraw-Hill Companies, Inc. Click here for terms of use. 2 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["source_attribution_in_option"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1feb7fbf3ab9","stem":"Two drugs act on the same tissue or organ via activation of different receptors, resulting in effects that are qualitatively the opposite of one another. An example would be the direct effects of norepinephrine and acetylcholine on heart rate. This represents which of the following types of antagonism?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemical"},{"label":"B","text":"Competitive"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispositional"},{"label":"D","text":"Pharmacologic"},{"label":"E","text":"Physiologic"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fbf6c92a5bf","stem":"We are repeatedly administering a drug orally. Every dose is 50 mg; the interval between doses is 8 h, which is identical to the drug’s plasma half- life. The bioavailability is 0.5. For as long as we conduct the experiment no interacting drugs are added or stopped, and there are no patient-related factors (affecting such things as absorption or elimination) that might change the drug’s pharmacokinetics. Which of the following formulas gives the best estimate of how long it will take for the drug to reach steady-state serum concentrations (CSS)? Abbreviations: AUC: area under the concentration-time curve Cl: clearance (mL/min)","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"D","text":"dose (mg)"},{"label":"F","text":"bioavailability (<1.0 for this drug given orally) ke: elimination rate constant t1/2: half-life (h) Vd: volume of distribution"},{"label":"A","text":"(0.693 ¥ Vd)/Cl"},{"label":"B","text":"1/ke"},{"label":"C","text":"4.5 ¥ t1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"(t1/2) ¥ (ke)"},{"label":"E","text":"D/(F ¥ t1/2) General Principles 3"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fe9c00435bba","stem":"We want to estimate, following drug administration, some measure that most reliably reflects the total amount of drug reaching the target tissue(s), over time. We’re giving the drug orally. Which of the following would pro- vide that measure best?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area under the blood concentration-time curve (AUC)"},{"label":"B","text":"Peak (maximum) blood concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"Product of the Vd and the first-order rate constant"},{"label":"D","text":"Time-to-peak blood concentration"},{"label":"E","text":"Vd"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42470ce3de3e","stem":"Experiments show that 95% of an oral 80-mg dose of Drug X is absorbed in a 70-kg test subject. However, because of extensive biotrans- formation during its first pass through the hepatic portal circulation, the bioavailability was only 0.25 (25%). Assuming a liver blood flow of 1500 mL/min, which of the following is the hepatic clearance of Drug X in this situation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60 mL/min"},{"label":"B","text":"375 mL/min"},{"label":"C","text":"740 mL/min"},{"label":"D","text":"1110 mL/min"},{"label":"E","text":"1425 mL/min"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5e4d5bb71919","stem":"A speaker at Grand Rounds is summarizing the literature on a very small subset of patients who develop acute hemolytic disease in response to drug therapy. The causative agents included common antimalarial drugs (chloroquine, quinine, and especially primaquine); cardiovascular drugs (hydralazine, procainamide, quinidine); and various antimicrobials (chlo- ramphenicol, nitrofurantoin, sulfonamide antibiotics). Which of the fol- lowing patient-related factors most likely accounts for their susceptibility to hemolysis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antimalarial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concurrently taking aspirin"},{"label":"B","text":"Genetically based glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency"},{"label":"C","text":"Primary renal disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Recently received chemotherapy with vincristine"},{"label":"E","text":"Serum cholinesterase deficiency 4 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4443b3504adb","stem":"We are conducting pharmacokinetic studies on a new drug that we hope will be approved for clinical use. We insert a venous catheter to sam- ple blood at various times after drug administration, and also take a sam- ple for the immediate predrug blood level of the drug (which should be zero). After assaying the blood samples for the drug we make a graph that plots serum drug concentrations over time, continuing until tests reveal blood levels as undetectable. Which of the following is calculated as the ratio of the area under the curve (AUC) obtained by oral administration vs. the AUC for intravenous administration of the same drug?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absorption"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"C","text":"Clearance"},{"label":"D","text":"Elimination rate constant"},{"label":"E","text":"Extraction ratio"},{"label":"F","text":"Volume of distribution"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e81b211f4846","stem":"We administer an acidic drug (A) with a pKa of 3.4 orally. But pH is 1.4, and plasma pH is 7.4. Assume the drug crosses membranes by simple pas- sive diffusion (e.g., no transporters are involved). Which of the following observations would be true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only ionized forms of the drug, A−, will be absorbed from the gut into the plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"The concentration ratio of total drug (A + HA−) would be 10,000:1 (gut > plasma)"},{"label":"C","text":"The drug will be hydrolyzed by a reaction with HCl, and so cannot be absorbed"},{"label":"D","text":"The drug will not be absorbed unless we raise gastric pH to equal pKa, as might be done with an antacid"},{"label":"E","text":"The drug would be absorbed, and at equilibrium the plasma concentration of the nonionized moiety (HA) would be 104 times higher than the plasma con- centration of A−. General Principles 5"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-68de4ee1cc9e","stem":"Identical doses of a drug are given orally ( ) and intravenously (•), we sample blood at various times, measure blood concentrations of the drug, and plot the data (shown in the figure). Further analysis of these data will allow us to determine which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elimination route(s)"},{"label":"B","text":"Extent of plasma protein binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Oral bioavailability"},{"label":"D","text":"Potency"},{"label":"E","text":"Therapeutic effectiveness"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ee47719745ce","stem":"The elimination of a drug is described as being heavily dependent on Phase II metabolic reactions. Which of the following is a Phase II reaction as far as drug elimination goes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucuronidation"},{"label":"B","text":"Deamination"},{"label":"C","text":"Ester hydrolysis"},{"label":"D","text":"Nitro reduction"},{"label":"E","text":"Sulfoxide formation 6 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c04168796f9d","stem":"We start intravenous infusion of a drug using a pump that ensures that the rate of drug delivery is constant over time. Which of the following fac- tors determines how long it will take for the drug to reach a steady-state concentration (CSS) in the blood?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apparent volume of distribution"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"C","text":"Clearance"},{"label":"D","text":"Half-life"},{"label":"E","text":"Infusion rate (mg of drug/min)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c82952ec9193","stem":"Yee et al. (Effect of grapefruit juice on blood cyclosporine concentra- tion; Lancet 345:955–956, 1995) examined several pharmacokinetic vari- ables related to oral cyclosporine administration with water, grapefruit juice, and orange juice: Grapefruit Juice Orange Juice Water p* AUC (ng•h/mL) 7057 ± 2172 4871 ± 2045 4932 ± 1451 <0.0001 Cmax (ng/mL) 1269 ± 381 972 ± 379 1080 ± 269 0.01 Tmax (hr) 2.86 ± 0.77 2.57 ± 0.85 2.36 ± 0.63 0.14 The numbers listed are arithmetic means ± one standard deviation of the mean. Cmax is the peak blood concentration, and Tmax is the time after administration at which peak serum concentrations of the drug are reached. p values are based on analysis of variance (ANOVA) corrected for repeated measures. These data, and what you should have learned from your basic pharma- cology studies, are most consistent with the hypothesis that grapefruit juice does which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acidifies the urine, favoring cyclosporine’s tubular reabsorption via a pH- dependent effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Activates an intestinal wall transporter for cyclosporine"},{"label":"C","text":"Alters the route(s) of elimination for cyclosporine"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits the first-pass metabolism of cyclosporine"},{"label":"E","text":"Reduces binding of cyclosporine to plasma proteins, thereby raising free (active) drug levels in the circulation General Principles 7"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fee3cf4d8bb6","stem":"A 60-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis will be started on a non- steroidal anti-inflammatory drug to suppress the joint inflammation. Pub- lished pharmacokinetic data for this drug include: Bioavailability (F): 1.0 (100%) Plasma half-life (t1/2): 0.5 h Volume of distribution (Vd): 45 L For this drug it is important to maintain an average steady-state concentration 2.0 mcg/mL in order to ensure adequate and continued anti-inflammatory activity. The drug will be given (taken) every 4 h. What dose will be needed to obtain an average steady-state drug con- centration of 2.0 mcg/mL?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 mg"},{"label":"B","text":"100 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"325 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"500 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"625 mg"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d307ad182ec","stem":"We take a blood sample from a patient (baseline measurement) and then administer Drug A intravenously. We take additional blood samples periodically thereafter and measure drug concentration in each sample. We repeat the experiment, this time giving the same drug orally. Then we plot the logarithm of drug concentration vs. time with data from both adminis- tration routes, and find to comparable elimination “curves” indicative of first-order elimination. What do the slopes of the resulting concentration vs. time curves tell us best about the pharmacokinetics of Drug A?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area under the curve (AUC)"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"C","text":"Elimination rate constant"},{"label":"D","text":"Extraction ratio"},{"label":"E","text":"Volume of distribution 8 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-043cfc6844c6","stem":"0 1.3 General Principles 9 Which of the following values comes closest to the elimination rate con- stant (ke) for this drug in this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.15 h−1"},{"label":"B","text":"0.22 h−1"},{"label":"C","text":"0.33 h−1"},{"label":"D","text":"0.60 h−1"},{"label":"E","text":"1.13 h−1"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-07467c08474c","stem":"When we evaluate new drugs in preclinical testing, one of many things we’d like to know is whether it’s largely confined to the vascular compart- ment (before it is eliminated) or distributes more widely. This is, of course, ultimately important to you, the clinician who may prescribe or adminis- ter the drug if it gets FDA approval. One way to get a handle on that is to calculate the apparent volume of distribution (Vd). Using the data for the hypothetical aminoglycoside, shown in Question 16, which of the following values comes closest to the apparent Vd for this drug?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.62 L"},{"label":"B","text":"19 L"},{"label":"C","text":"50 L"},{"label":"D","text":"110 L"},{"label":"E","text":"350 L"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1104508b792","stem":"When evaluating the effects of certain adrenergic agonists in a variety of in vitro and in vivo models, we find that the responses exhibit the phe- nomenon of tachyphylaxis. Which of the following best describes what the term tachyphylaxis means?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in the rate of the response, for example, an increase of the rate of muscle contraction"},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate hypersensitivity reactions (i.e., anaphylaxis)"},{"label":"C","text":"Prompt conformational changes of the receptor such that agonists, but not antagonists, are able to bind and cause a response"},{"label":"D","text":"Quick and progressive rises in the intensity of drug response, with repeated administration, even when the doses are unchanged"},{"label":"E","text":"Rapid development of tolerance to the drug’s effects 10 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d39d73cc6693","stem":"9% NaCl, and we will use this to administer intermittent injections of the drug every 4 h. The target blood level of the drug, following each injection, is 8 mcg/mL. With this plan in mind, which of the following comes closest to the dose that should be administered every 4 h?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.960 mg (or 1 mg)"},{"label":"B","text":"6.4 mg (or 6 mg)"},{"label":"C","text":"25.6 mg (or 25 mg)"},{"label":"D","text":"150 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"550 mg"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3182e6fe049f","stem":"A patient is experiencing severe postoperative pain, and we need to give a loading dose of an analgesic drug for prompt relief of discomfort. The drug we choose has the same pharmacokinetic properties as the one described in Question 19: Half-life: 12 h Clearance: 0.08 L/min Volume of distribution: 60 L Our target serum concentration for the drug is 8 mcg/mL. Which of the following comes closest to the correct loading dose?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.48 mg (rounded to 0.5 mg)"},{"label":"B","text":"150 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"320 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"480 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"640 mg General Principles 11"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3072f28a6275","stem":"We administer a highly lipid-soluble drug and monitor its elimination in vivo and in vitro. All the data indicate that it is transformed to a variety of more polar metabolites by a group of heme proteins that activate molecu- lar oxygen to a form that is capable of interacting with organic substrates such as our test drug. Which of the following is the most likely enzyme or enzyme system involved in the initial metabolism of our test drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Lipids","topicOriginal":"Lipids","subtopic":"Lipid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclooxygenase"},{"label":"B","text":"Cytochrome P450s"},{"label":"C","text":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH)"},{"label":"E","text":"UDP–glucuronosyltransferase 12 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Lipids","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-7c253e32f7da","stem":"after giving acebutolol, followed by exercise at the same level used in condition 2 Acebutolol given at rest causes a slight but consistent increase of heart rate. Give a bigger dose at rest and heart rate rises a bit more. When the patient exercises after receiving a low dose acebutolol, heart rate rises significantly less than it did in the absence of acebutolol. With exercise after the higher dose of acebutolol, the tachycardia is blunted even more. The figure summarizes the main findings. General Principles 13 Which of the following statements best summarizes the actions of acebutolol?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has higher affinity for adrenergic receptors than the endogenous agonists, epi- nephrine, and norepinephrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Is a partial agonist for β-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Is activating spare receptors on myocardial cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Is an irreversible or noncompetitive β blocker"},{"label":"E","text":"Is changing conformation of the adrenergic receptors"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-cf862c394b6b","stem":"35 h−1. No loading dose will be given. Approximately how long will it take for blood levels to reach steady state after the infusion begins?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.7 h"},{"label":"B","text":"1.2 h"},{"label":"C","text":"3.5 h"},{"label":"D","text":"9 h"},{"label":"E","text":"24 h"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6363397ed2c4","stem":"A patient who is supposed to be taking a drug once a day gets con- fused and for a couple of days takes excessive daily doses, leading to toxic- ity. The drug has a mean plasma half-life of 40 h. Right now the patient’s plasma concentration of the drug is 6 mcg/mL. Although what to do next will depend on actual blood tests for drug levels, the usual plan in this case is to have the patient skip one or several daily doses of the drug until blood levels first enter the therapeutic and nontoxic range, which in this case is 0.8 mcg/mL. How many daily doses should be withheld?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"},{"label":"E","text":"5 14 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-18ad408b8486","stem":"We want to calculate the apparent volume of distribution (Vd) for a hypothetical drug (Drug A) that has a half-life of 4 h. All (100%) of an absorbed dose of this drug undergoes Phase I oxidation, followed by con- jugation (Phase II reaction). We rapidly inject a known dose, and 30 min later begin taking serial blood samples (30 min apart) and quantifying drug concentration in each sample. Which of the following information must we measure or other- wise determine to calculate Vd in the easiest possible way?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area under the drug concentration-time curve (AUC)"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"C","text":"Clearance"},{"label":"D","text":"Elimination rate constant (kel)"},{"label":"E","text":"Maximum blood concentration immediately after the bolus injection (C0)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb1e9a64d506","stem":"A patient with a bacterial infection requires intravenous antibiotic therapy. The chosen drug has a clearance (Cl) of 70 mL/min. The apparent volume of distribution (Vd) is 50 L. The plan is to administer the drug intravenously every 6 h and achieve a 4-mg/L steady-state blood level of the drug. No loading dose strategy is to be used. Which of the following maintenance doses is needed to achieve this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 mg"},{"label":"B","text":"24 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"100 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"300 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"1200 mg General Principles 15"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a1ff07a6508e","stem":"We are working with a pharmacologically inert but easily measured substance, X. Its elimination shows linear kinetics (first-order plot of log drug concentration vs. time during elimination is a straight line). The plasma half-life is 30 min. Bolus IV doses well in excess of 100 mg must be given in order to saturate the enzymes responsible for metabolizing the drug, which will then lead to zero-order elimination kinetics. We infuse a solution of X intravenously. The concentration of the solu- tion is 2 mg/mL; the infusion rate is 1 mL/min and is kept constant at that. We continue the infusion for 24 h. After allowing ample time for the drug to be eliminated completely, we repeat the administration. This time the concentration of the solution of X is 4 mg/mL, and we infuse it at a rate of 2 mL/min. Which of the following other variables will also be changed as a result of the stated changes to the infusion protocol?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elimination rate constant"},{"label":"B","text":"Half-life"},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma concentration when CSS is reached"},{"label":"D","text":"Time to reach steady-state concentration (CSS)"},{"label":"E","text":"Total body clearance"},{"label":"F","text":"Volume of distribution"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-618cadd2b582","stem":"A new drug, Drug A, undergoes a series of Phase I metabolic reactions before its metabolites ultimately are eliminated. Which of the following statements best describes the characteristics of Drug A, or the role of Phase I reactions in its metabolism or actions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete metabolism of Drug A by Phase I reactions will yield products that are less likely to undergo renal tubular reabsorption"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug A is a very polar substance"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug A will be biologically inactive until it is metabolized"},{"label":"D","text":"Phase I metabolism of Drug A involves conjugation, as with glucuronic acid or sulfate"},{"label":"E","text":"Phase I metabolism of Drug A will increase its intracellular access and actions 16 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-cd58368d6d79","stem":"Dopamine, epinephrine (or norepinephrine), and histamine are impor- tant neurotransmitter agonists. When these ligands interact with their cellu- lar receptors, how do they mainly elicit their responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activating adenylyl cyclase, leading to increased intracellular cAMP levels"},{"label":"B","text":"Activating phospholipase C"},{"label":"C","text":"Inducing or inhibiting synthesis of ligand-specific intracellular proteins"},{"label":"D","text":"Opening or closing ligand-gated ion channels"},{"label":"E","text":"Regulating intracellular second messengers through G protein–coupled receptors"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cf54de79f548","stem":"The FDA assigns the letters A, B, C, D, and X to drugs approved for human use. To which of the following does this classification apply?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amount of dosage reduction needed as serum creatinine clearances fall"},{"label":"B","text":"Amount of dosage reduction needed in presence of liver dysfunction"},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal risk when given to pregnant women"},{"label":"D","text":"Relative margins of safety/therapeutic index"},{"label":"E","text":"The number of unlabeled uses for a drug"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0deda1c0e7e","stem":"The Food and Drug Administration has regulatory authority over pre- scription drugs, OTC drugs, and nutritional supplements (herbals and other so-called nutriceuticals). Such authority includes approval, market- ing (advertising), and withdrawal of drugs from the market. Which of the following statements about these regulatory matters is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drugs approved for sale OTC first received FDA approval for sale and market- ing “by prescription only”"},{"label":"B","text":"If a pharmaceutical manufacturer provides data sufficient to obtain FDA approval for sale by prescription, the manufacturer is then allowed to sell the drug over-the-counter (OTC)"},{"label":"C","text":"If the FDA approves a prescription drug for sale (prescribing), the MD can pre- scribe the drug only for the FDA-approved indication (use)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nutritional supplements can be marketed without providing proof of efficacy or safety to the FDA"},{"label":"E","text":"Phase III testing of prescription drugs that have been approved by the FDA gives complete information about adverse responses and pertinent drug-drug interactions General Principles Answers"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b9cf877aa1ab","stem":"The answer is d. (Brunton, pp 131–135; Craig, pp 785–787; Katzung, pp 65–73.) One of the most controversial (if not mind-boggling) aspects of marketing herbals, nutritional supplements, and other “nutriceuticals,” is that manufacturers and sellers have to provide no prior or scientific proof of General Principles Answers 37 safety or efficacy to the FDA—something that must be provided, and with abundant and scientifically sound data, before the FDA will approve a drug for sale by prescription. Herbals, for example, came under the umbrella of the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994. Such products, you may have noted, come with labels that state “serv- ing size,” rather than dose, as if we are going to be ingesting some carrots instead of a potentially active and dangerous drug. Sellers of such products as herbals cannot make explicit claims that their products will prevent, diagnose, treat, or cure any disease. DHSEA allows a seller of a nutritional supplement to say such obtuse things as “boosts your memory,” or “improves urinary tract health,” but they cannot state “helps prevent or treat Alzheimer’s disease” or “reduces urinary fre- quency and nocturia if you have benign prostatic hypertrophy.” In fact, DSHEA simply requires that the label on the product states, in essence, that the FDA hasn’t evaluated any of the claims the manufacturer has made, plainly or implicitly, on the label. Moreover, while a drug company must provide and pay for the oblig- atory preclinical and clinical studies before a prescription drug gets the OK, if there is a reason or attempt to pull a nutriceutical from the market the FDA must do the work and pay the costs to prove that the product is unsafe or ineffective. OTC drugs needn’t be preapproved by the FDA as prescription drugs (answer a). While such prior approval is the trend, if not the norm (e.g., consider many antihistamines for allergy or insomnia; or drugs that inhibit gastric acid secretion being marketed for “heartburn” or “acid indigestion”), consider aspirin. It’s been available OTC for decades and for its common uses (pain, fever, inflammation) it has never gotten an FDA blessing. Indeed, some say that if aspirin was being considered for approval as a pre- scription drug today, it would not be approved because of the risks of seri- ous adverse responses (e.g., bleeding tendencies from antiplatelet effects; bronchospasm in many asthmatics) and drug-drug interactions. Nonethe- less, we have many OTC drugs nowadays that first got approval for sale by prescription only . . . antihistamines, inhibitors of gastric acid secretion (noted above), and others. Approval for OTC sale was petitioned by the manufacturer, and then approved only after reviews by and recommenda- tions from FDA expert advisory panels. Answer c is incorrect. Once a drug gets approval for sale and use as a prescription drug, and for a given indication (or more, depending on data 38 Pharmacology submitted and approved), the physician can prescribe that drug for any (reasonable) purpose for which he or she thinks is appropriate, safe, and effective. Physicians and scientists learn or hypothesize about new uses for a drug, conduct clinical studies, and overall can legally prescribe drugs for the off-label or unlabeled uses. Phase III testing of drugs is conducted in usually no more than 5000 patients with a disease for which the drug is to be marketed. The main goal is to assess effectiveness for the stated use; and for overall safety. These are rather tightly controlled studies. The number of subjects, and the conditions in which the drug is given, are quite different from what happens in the “real world”—including Phase IV, which is the postmarketing surveillance phase of drug approval, where tens or hundreds of thousands of patients may get the drug in largely uncontrolled circumstances. Many drugs have come (been FDA-approved) and gone (pulled from the market because of adverse reactions or interactions, including those leading to death) because of what we’ve learned after the drug was approved by the FDA. The Peripheral Nervous Systems: Autonomic and Somatic Adrenergic agonists Ganglionic blockers Adrenergic antagonists Monoamine oxidase inhibitors Cholinergic agonists Skeletal muscle relaxants Cholinergic antagonists Skeletal neuromuscular blockers Questions DIRECTIONS: Each item contains a question or incomplete statement that is followed by several responses. Select the one best response to each question. Questions 32–37 [Note: The first seven questions in this chapter are based on a schematic diagram of the peripheral nervous system. I think this approach is the most appropriate, efficient, and briefest way to help you assess, or clarify, some essential “high yield” aspects of autonomic anatomy, (neuro)physiology, and pharmacology based on a few main “rules” and key exceptions to them. I hope this approach will be beneficial.] The diagram shows, in schematized fashion, the main efferent pathways in the peripheral nervous systems—the autonomic (parasympathetic and sympa- thetic; PNS, SNS, respectively) and somatic nervous systems—“from the CNS/spinal cord (at the far left, not labeled) out” to peripheral target (effectors). Those nerves are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preganglionic parasympathetic"},{"label":"B","text":"Postganglionic parasympathetic"},{"label":"C","text":"Preganglionic sympathetic"},{"label":"D","text":"Postganglionic sympathetic to structures other than sweat glands"},{"label":"E","text":"“Preganglionic” (functional equivalence) sympathetic to the adrenal (suprarenal) medulla"},{"label":"F","text":"Preganglionic sympathetic, sweat gland innervation"},{"label":"G","text":"Postganglionic sympathetic to sweat glands"},{"label":"H","text":"Motor nerve, somatic nervous system 39 Copyright © 2007 by The McGraw-Hill Companies, Inc. Click here for terms of use. 40 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","source_attribution_in_option","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2fc57e71f57c","stem":"Anatomic, neurochemical, and pharmacologic studies of nerves a, c, e, f, and h indicate that they share one common property that is important to your understanding of peripheral nervous system pharmacology. Which of the following statements best summarizes what that property is?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are cholinergic, activate postsynaptic nicotinic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Are adrenergic, but receptor type on target cell depends on what/where the cell is"},{"label":"C","text":"Cannot release their neurotransmitter(s) in the presence of atropine"},{"label":"D","text":"Have the ability to directly activate all the adrenergic and all the cholinergic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Recycle their neurotransmitter after each action potential, rather than synthe- sizing new transmitter de novo The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 41"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d627f7fab204","stem":"Similar multidisciplinary assessments of nerve d, a “typical” postgan- glionic sympathetic nerve, indicate that it is quite different from all the other nerves shown in the schematic of the peripheral nervous systems. Which of the following statements accurately describes that difference?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine selectively blocks activation of receptors by the neurotransmitter released from nerve d"},{"label":"B","text":"It causes bronchodilation (airway smooth muscle relaxation) when it is activated"},{"label":"C","text":"It is adrenergic (or noradrenergic if you wish to use that term instead)"},{"label":"D","text":"The primary neurotransmitter synthesized by nerve d is epinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"When nerve d is physiologically activated by an action potential, actions of its released neurotransmitter are terminated mainly by hydrolysis in the synaptic cleft"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b384ddfcc609","stem":"Reuptake (into the nerve) is the main physiologic process for termi- nating the postsynaptic activity of a peripheral nervous system neurotrans- mitter. To which nerve does this process apply?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a"},{"label":"B","text":"b"},{"label":"C","text":"c"},{"label":"D","text":"d"},{"label":"E","text":"e"},{"label":"F","text":"f"},{"label":"G","text":"g"},{"label":"H","text":"h"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39fc564ca15a","stem":"Nerve d, the typical postganglionic sympathetic nerve, is activated by a normally generated action potential. On which of the following receptor types does the neurotransmitter it releases act?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1 Adrenergic"},{"label":"B","text":"α2 Adrenergic"},{"label":"C","text":"β1 Adrenergic"},{"label":"D","text":"β2 Adrenergic"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscarinic"},{"label":"F","text":"Nicotinic"},{"label":"G","text":"It depends on the target tissue (effector) type 42 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-32fd08ae0e45","stem":"Your studies also reveal that something is unique about nerve g, the postganglionic fibers that innervate sweat glands. Which of the following descriptive phrases best identifies that difference?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cocaine blocks release of its neurotransmitter"},{"label":"B","text":"Is adrenergic"},{"label":"C","text":"Is cholinergic, but anatomically and functionally part of the SNS"},{"label":"D","text":"Its neurotransmitter acts on nicotinic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Uses epinephrine as its neurotransmitter"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2ce3452bf385","stem":"Nerve g, the postganglionic nerve innervating sweat glands, is activated by a normally generated action potential. On which of the following receptor types does the neurotransmitter it releases act?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1 Adrenergic"},{"label":"B","text":"α2 Adrenergic"},{"label":"C","text":"Muscarinic"},{"label":"D","text":"Nicotinic"},{"label":"E","text":"β1 Adrenergic"},{"label":"F","text":"β2 Adrenergic"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-705e4dbbec5c","stem":"Assume that all the efferent pathways in the schematic are tonically active (a quite reasonable assumption), even if at low levels. We add tubocurarine or pancuronium to the system, and as expected it blocks neu- rotransmitter activation of certain structures. Which of the following nerves innervates those structures and normally activates them in the absence of these or similar drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a"},{"label":"B","text":"b"},{"label":"C","text":"c"},{"label":"D","text":"d"},{"label":"E","text":"e"},{"label":"F","text":"f"},{"label":"G","text":"g"},{"label":"H","text":"h The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 43 Questions 39–45 The figure shows some of the main elements of norepinephrine (NE) syn- thesis, release, actions, and other steps in adrenergic neurotransmission. You do not need the diagram to answer this series of questions, but I do not think that showing it gives you an unfair advantage—just a little memory jolt and the “big picture”—that may aid your answering or reviewing. Note that the effector (target) cell on the far right will have either an α-adrenergic receptor or a β-adrenergic receptor, depending on what the effector cell is."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","too_many_options","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-01f49f51dd4f","stem":"Mitochondria in the terminus of adrenergic nerve endings contain an abundance of monoamine oxidase (MAO). Which of the following best summarizes the biological role of that enzyme at that site?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drives storage vesicles that contain NE to the nerve “ending” so that exocytotic release can occur in response to an action potential"},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolically degrades NE that is free (not stored in vesicles) in the nerve terminal"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolizes dopamine to NE"},{"label":"D","text":"Provides metabolic energy for nonexocytotic release of NE in response to amphetamines and other catecholamine-releasing drugs"},{"label":"E","text":"Synthesizes ATP that is required to transport free intraneuronal NE into the storage granules/vesicles 44 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-68662b27a3d0","stem":"Ultrastructural studies, combined with suitable histochemical tech- niques, show that the bulk of NE in the normal resting adrenergic neuron is stored in membrane-bound vesicles or granules. We administer a drug that, over time, depletes this supply of neurotransmitter and decreases the responses to sympathetic nerve activation. In vitro studies with chromaffin cells (dispersed cells from the suprarenal medulla) reveal that the drug acts by inhibiting uptake of the NE into the vesicles; it has no direct effect on catecholamine synthesis. Which of the following drugs fits this description best?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pargyline"},{"label":"B","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"C","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Reserpine"},{"label":"E","text":"Tyramine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-168b1e7945c5","stem":"A substance abuser self-administers cocaine and experiences a variety of significant changes in cardiovascular function, in addition to the CNS- stimulating effects for which the drug is used. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism by which the cocaine causes its main peripheral and CNS effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of α2-adrenergic receptors leading to increased NE release"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockage of NE reuptake via the amine pump"},{"label":"C","text":"Direct activation postsynaptic α- and β-adrenergic receptors, leading to sympa- thomimetic (adrenomimetic) responses"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of MAO, leading to increased intraneuronal NE levels"},{"label":"E","text":"Prevention of NE exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-918239bd2303","stem":"We administer a drug that is a selective agonist at the presynaptic α- receptors (α2) in the peripheral sympathetic nervous system. It has no effect on α1 receptors, β receptors, or any other ligand receptors that are important in peripheral nervous system function. Which of the following is the only response that is likely to occur?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of NE exocytosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of the amine pump, stimulation of NE reuptake"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of dopamine β-hydroxylase, the enzyme that converts intraneuronal dopamine to NE"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of NE release in response to an action potential"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of intraneuronal MAO The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 45"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-7385ff11ca1e","stem":"We administer a therapeutic dose of a drug that selectively and com- petitively blocks the postsynaptic α-adrenergic (α1) receptors. It has no effects on presynaptic α-adrenergic receptors (α2) or β-adrenergic recep- tors found anywhere in the periphery, whether as an agonist or antagonist. Which of the following is the most likely drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ephedrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Labetalol"},{"label":"C","text":"Phentolamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Prazosin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f568e2529c8b","stem":"Oxybutynin is indicated for the treatment of overactive bladder, reducing the symptoms of “urge incontinence,” urinary urgency, and frequent urination. It prevents physiologic activation of the bladder’s detrusor and simultaneously prevents relaxation of the sphincter. Side effects include constipation, dry mouth, blurred vision, photophobia, urinary retention, and slight increase in heart rate. The manufacturer also advises: “Heat stroke and fever due to decreased sweating in hot temperatures have been reported.” Oxybutynin has no direct effect on blood vessels that might change blood pressure. Based on this information, which of the following prototype drugs is most like oxybutynin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"β-Adrenergic blockers (e.g., propranolol)"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phentolamine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b18641747072","stem":"A morbidly obese person visits the local bariatric (weight loss) clinic seeking a pill that will help shed weight. The physician prescribes dex- troamphetamine. In addition to causing its expected centrally mediated anorexigenic (appetite-suppressant) effects it causes a host of peripheral adrenergic effects that, for some patients, can prove fatal. Which of the fol- lowing best summarizes the main mechanism by which dextroampheta- mine, or amphetamines in general, cause their peripheral autonomic effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activates MAO"},{"label":"B","text":"Blocks NE reuptake via the amine pump/transporter"},{"label":"C","text":"Displaces, releases, intraneuronal NE"},{"label":"D","text":"Enhances NE synthesis, leading to massive neurotransmitter overproduction"},{"label":"E","text":"Stabilizes the adrenergic nerve ending by directly activating α2 receptors 46 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6ed2a0c81ad2","stem":"A patient receives echothiophate during eye surgery. Which of the fol- lowing enzymes is affected by this autonomic drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyrosine hydroxylase—stimulated"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)—inhibited"},{"label":"C","text":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)—inhibited"},{"label":"D","text":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)—stimulated"},{"label":"E","text":"DOPA decarboxylase—stimulated"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d80b1f633609","stem":"We want to prescribe scopolamine, as a transdermal drug delivery sys- tem (skin patch), for a patient who will be leaving for an expensive cruise and is very susceptible to motion sickness. Which of the following comor- bidities would weigh against prescribing the drug because it is most likely to pose adverse effects—or be truly contraindicated?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angle-closure (narrow-angle) glaucoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Bradycardia"},{"label":"C","text":"History shellfish allergies"},{"label":"D","text":"Resting blood pressure of 112/70"},{"label":"E","text":"Hypothyroidism, mild"},{"label":"F","text":"Parkinson’s disease (early onset, not currently treated)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cce9e1edb9ab","stem":"We have a patient with essential hypertension, and unusually high cir- culating catecholamine levels. Our goal is to block both α- and β-adrenergic receptors using just one drug. Which of the following is capable of doing that?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labetalol"},{"label":"B","text":"Metoprolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Nadolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Pindolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Timolol The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 47"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fbbbe8f6d587","stem":"A man who has been “surfing the Web” in search of an aphrodisiac or some other agent to enhance “sexual prowess and performance” discovers yohimbine. He consumes the drug in excess and develops symptoms of toxicity that require your intervention. You consult your preferred drug ref- erence and learn that yohimbine is a selective α2-adrenergic antagonist. Which of the following should you expect as a response to this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bradycardia"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchoconstriction"},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive secretions by exocrine glands (salivary, lacrimal, etc.)"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertension"},{"label":"E","text":"Reduced cardiac output from reduced left ventricular contractility"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d3fcb571bf18","stem":"A patient with Alzheimer’s disease is taking an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor specifically approved for that indication, primarily because it is quite lipophilic and so enters the CNS well. Which of the following drugs is the patient most likely receiving?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tacrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Edrophonium"},{"label":"C","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Ambenonium"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3c6a71da5386","stem":"A child overdoses on a drug that affects both the autonomic and somatic nervous systems. As blood level of the drug rises he experiences hyperten- sion and tachycardia, accompanied by skeletal muscle tremor. Further eleva- tions of the drug cause all the expected signs and symptoms of autonomic ganglionic blockade, plus weakness and eventual paralysis of skeletal mus- cle. Which of the following drugs did the child most likely ingest?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bethanechol"},{"label":"B","text":"Nicotine"},{"label":"C","text":"Pilocarpine"},{"label":"D","text":"Scopolamine"},{"label":"E","text":"Tubocurarine 48 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ea35b2c44599","stem":"Review of a patient’s chart reveals that two years ago she was treated with ritodrine. Which of the following was the most likely condition or rea- son for which this drug was given?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parkinson’s disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchial asthma"},{"label":"C","text":"Depression"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertension"},{"label":"E","text":"Premature labor"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-94c08a712510","stem":"A patient receives bethanechol after abdominal surgery. Her heart rate falls slightly and she experiences some wheezing. Which of the following best accounts for or explains these cardiac and pulmonary responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Expected side effects"},{"label":"B","text":"Idiosyncrasy"},{"label":"C","text":"Parasympathetic ganglionic activation"},{"label":"D","text":"Reflex (baroreceptor) suppression of cardiac rate"},{"label":"E","text":"Undiagnosed asthma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-77a7dfd3767d","stem":"A 35-year-old man who weighs 150 pounds and is 5 feet 10 inches tall is transported to the emergency department in severe distress. He com- plains of episodes of severe, throbbing headaches, profuse diaphoresis, and palpitations. Eighteen months ago his physician told him he is healthy except for what is assumed to be Stage 2 essential hypertension, but he refused medication and has not seen a health care provider for the last year and a half. He denies use of any drugs, whether prescription or over-the- counter, legal or otherwise. Assessment now reveals that he is tachycardic and has an irregular pulse (sinus tachycardia, with occasional premature ventricular beats, are noted on his EKG). Heart rate at rest is approximately 130 beats/min, sometimes more. His resting blood pressure is approximately 200/140 mm Hg. These cardiovascular findings are shown in the figure. The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 49 The first year house officer who is caring for this patient knows that all the orally effective β-adrenergic blockers are approved for use to treat essential hypertension, and concludes that prompt lowering of blood pressure is essential for this patient. Therefore, he orders intravenous administration of propranolol (at the arrow, in the figure), and a large dose of the drug since the symptoms seem severe. Unknown to the MD is the fact that the patient’s signs and symptoms are due to a pheochromocytoma (epinephrine-secreting tumor of the adrenal/ suprarenal medulla). Which of the following statements best describes the most likely ulti- mate outcome of administering this β blocker (or any other β blocker that lacks α-blocking activity), supplemented with no other medication, to this patient with an undiagnosed pheochromocytoma?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart failure, cardiogenic shock, death"},{"label":"B","text":"Long-lasting normalization of heart rate, contractility, and blood pressure"},{"label":"C","text":"Normalization of blood pressures but persistence of tachycardia"},{"label":"D","text":"Restoration of normal sinus rate and rhythm, but no change of blood pressure from predrug levels."},{"label":"E","text":"Sudden and significant rise of systolic blood pressure and heart rate"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-5d8c8f6bba3e","stem":"The house officer considers prescribing nadolol for a 53-year-old patient. Which of the following preexisting conditions (comorbidities) would most likely contraindicate safe use of this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angina pectoris, chronic-stable (effort-induced)"},{"label":"B","text":"Asthma"},{"label":"C","text":"Essential hypertension"},{"label":"D","text":"Heart failure, mild"},{"label":"E","text":"Sinus tachychardia 50 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c88fa33115d5","stem":"A variety of ophthalmic drugs, working by several main mechanisms of action, are useful for managing chronic open-angle glaucoma. Which of the following reduces intraocular pressure by decreasing the formation of aqueous humor, rather than by changing the size of the pupil(s)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Timolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Echothiophate"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoflurophate"},{"label":"D","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e728357acee6","stem":"We give an “effective dose” of atropine to a person who was poisoned with an AChE inhibitor. Which of the following structures will continue to be overactivated by the excess ACh after the atropine is given?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Airway smooth muscle"},{"label":"B","text":"SA node of the heart"},{"label":"C","text":"Salivary and lacrimal glands"},{"label":"D","text":"Skeletal muscle"},{"label":"E","text":"Vascular smooth muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb275c1aac41","stem":"Guanadrel is an antihypertensive drug: it reduces arteriolar constric- tion, and in doing so lowers blood pressure, by reducing the amount of norepinephrine in peripheral adrenergic nerves. Lowered blood pressure is not accompanied by reflex tachycardia, and in fact a reduction of heart rate from predrug levels is the more common outcome. The main ocular effect of guanadrel is miosis. The widespread abolition of sympathetic influences throughout the body often causes diarrhea and urinary frequency. Based on this description, to which of the following drugs is guanadrel most similar in terms of its ultimate qualitative autonomic effects, but not specifically in terms of mechanism of action?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"label":"B","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"C","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"E","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"F","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"G","text":"Reserpine The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 51"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-592b5d8be3ee","stem":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD, e.g., emphysema) is receiving an orally inhaled muscarinic receptor-blocking drug to maintain bronchodilation. Which of the following drug is this patient most likely taking?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"B","text":"Diphenhydramine"},{"label":"C","text":"Ipratropium"},{"label":"D","text":"Pancuronium"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7bce0f9aaf29","stem":"We have a 48-year-old female patient with a history of myasthenia gravis. She has been treated with an oral acetylcholinesterase inhibitor for several years, and has done well till now. She presents with muscle weak- ness and other signs and symptoms that could reflect either a cholinergic crisis (excess dosages of her maintenance drug) or a myasthenic crisis (insufficient treatment). We will use a rapidly acting parenteral acetyl- cholinesterase inhibitor (AChE) to help make the differential diagnosis. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this use?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edrophonium"},{"label":"B","text":"Malathion"},{"label":"C","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Pralidoxime"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyridostigmine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f177514200ce","stem":"It is common to include small amounts of epinephrine (EPI) in solu- tions of local anesthetics that will be administered by infiltration (injection around sensory nerve endings), as when a skin laceration needs suturing. Which of the following is the most likely reason for, or outcome of, includ- ing the EPI?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"To antagonize the otherwise intense and common vasoconstrictor and hyper- tensive effects of the anesthetic"},{"label":"B","text":"To counteract cardiac depression caused by the anesthetic"},{"label":"C","text":"To prevent anaphylaxis in patients who are allergic to the anesthetic"},{"label":"D","text":"To reduce the risk of toxicity caused by systemic absorption of the anesthetic"},{"label":"E","text":"To shorten the duration of anesthetic action 52 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9b493b43f77b","stem":"A patient presents with an anaphylactic reaction following a bee sting. Which of the following is the drug of choice for treating the multiple car- diovascular and pulmonary problems that, if not promptly corrected, could lead to the patient’s death?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Diphenhydramine"},{"label":"C","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"E","text":"Norepinephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f7ea250aa0d5","stem":"Phentolamine and prazosin are, basically, members of the same drug class. Which of the following properties or characteristics applies to both drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are competitive antagonists at α-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Profoundly inhibit gastric acid secretion"},{"label":"C","text":"Cause a high incidence of bronchoconstriction in asthmatics"},{"label":"D","text":"Cause bradycardia"},{"label":"E","text":"Are used chronically for the treatment of primary hypotension"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71ffbb6df236","stem":"Left ventricular contractile force improves when dobutamine is given to a 60-year-old man with low cardiac output due to reductions of both heart rate and stroke volume. By which of the following adrenergic receptor- mediated actions is dobutamine causing these effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-Adrenergic agonist"},{"label":"B","text":"α-Adrenergic antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"β1-Adrenergic agonist"},{"label":"D","text":"β1-Adrenergic antagonist"},{"label":"E","text":"Mixed α and β agonist"},{"label":"F","text":"Mixed α and β antagonist The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 53"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-26b73a4fd7a1","stem":"It’s fair to say that epinephrine, norepinephrine, and acetylcholine play the most important roles as agonists for the various receptors under control of the peripheral nervous systems. However, dopamine also plays a small but important role, particularly when administered IV at low doses. Which of the following functions in the periphery are affected by this catecholamine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchodilation via relaxation of airway smooth muscles"},{"label":"B","text":"Direct activation of pressure receptors (e.g., baroreceptors) in response to blood pressure changes triggered by other agonists"},{"label":"C","text":"Direct activation of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, release of aldosterone"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of epinephrine release from chromaffin cells (e.g., cells of the adrenal/suprarenal medulla)"},{"label":"E","text":"Regulation of renal blood flow via control of renal arterial tone"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb3147c44e3b","stem":"We observe a rise of arterial pulse pressure during slow IV infusion of a drug to a normal subject. Which of the following drugs most likely caused this response?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"B","text":"Amphetamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Metoprolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a42fe4e6a6b","stem":"We are using novel in vitro methods to follow the fate and postsynap- tic actions of norepinephrine, released upon an action potential generated in an adrenergic nerve. An action potential is generated and the postsynap- tic effector briefly responds. Milliseconds later, the response is over. Which of the following processes mainly accounted for the brevity of the response, and termination of the released NE’s actions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Physiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolism by enzyme(s) located near the postsynaptic receptor(s) and/or in the synaptic cleft"},{"label":"B","text":"Reuptake into the nerve ending"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolism by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)"},{"label":"D","text":"Degradation by mitochondrial monoamine oxidase (MAO)"},{"label":"E","text":"Conversion to a “false neurotransmitter” in the nerve ending 54 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Physiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-dce3db04a313","stem":"We are contemplating administration of a nonselective β-adrenergic blocker to a patient. In which of the following conditions is this considered generally acceptable, appropriate, and safe?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angina, vasospastic (“variant”; Prinzmetal’s)"},{"label":"B","text":"Asthma"},{"label":"C","text":"Bradycardia"},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetes mellitus, insulin-dependent and poorly controlled"},{"label":"E","text":"Heart block (second degree or greater)"},{"label":"F","text":"Hyperthyroidism, symptomatic and acute"},{"label":"G","text":"Severe congestive heart failure"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d73928cac6fc","stem":"A patient presents in the emergency department in great distress and with the following signs and symptoms: bizarre behavior, delirium facial flushing clear lungs, no wheezing, rales, etc. high heart rate absence of bowel sounds distended abdomen, full bladder hot, dry skin absence of lacrimal, salivary secretions very high fever dilated pupils that do not respond to light Which of the following drugs has most likely caused these signs and symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"AChE inhibitors"},{"label":"B","text":"α-Adrenergic blockers"},{"label":"C","text":"Antimuscarinics"},{"label":"D","text":"β-Adrenergic blockers"},{"label":"E","text":"Parasympathomimetics (muscarinic agonists)"},{"label":"F","text":"Peripherally acting (neuronal) catecholamine depletors The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 55"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-054810fbbe3b","stem":"Acebutolol and pindolol are classified as β blockers with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA). In a practical sense, what does this mean?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are partial agonists (mixed agonist/antagonists)"},{"label":"B","text":"Cause norepinephrine and epinephrine release"},{"label":"C","text":"Induce catecholamine synthesis"},{"label":"D","text":"Potentiate the actions of norepinephrine on α-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Useful when cardiac positive inotropic and chronotropic effects are wanted"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-93cfdd65e892","stem":"A 10-year-old boy is diagnosed with Attention Deficit/Attention Deficit- Hyperactivity disorder (ADD/ADHD). Which of the following drugs is most likely to prove effective for relieving the boy’s main symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobutamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Methylphenidate"},{"label":"C","text":"Pancuronium"},{"label":"D","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"E","text":"Scopolamine"},{"label":"F","text":"Terbutaline"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-07b699e2866f","stem":"During surgery we administer hexamethonium to an anesthetized patient. Which of the following effects should you expect in response to this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bradycardia mediated by activation of the baroreceptor reflex"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased GI tract motility, possible spontaneous defecation"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased salivary secretions"},{"label":"D","text":"Miosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Vasodilation"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d0b460a8bf3","stem":"A patient with chronic open-angle glaucoma is treated with a topical ophthalmic β blocker. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism by which this drug lowers intraocular pressure?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contracting the circular pupillary constrictor muscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Contracting the iris dilator muscles"},{"label":"C","text":"Decreasing aqueous humor synthesis/secretion"},{"label":"D","text":"Dilating the uveoscleral veins"},{"label":"E","text":"Directly opening the trabecular meshwork 56 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c1081f96deeb","stem":"A 65-year-old man is losing his vision. Retinal examination reveals optic nerve cupping. Visual field tests show peripheral vision loss, and tonometry shows that his intraocular pressure is increased. Following drug treatment he has improved visual acuity and near-normal intraocular pres- sure. Which of the following drugs was most likely given?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baclofen"},{"label":"B","text":"Homatropine"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Scopolamine"},{"label":"E","text":"Timolol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-72c702813106","stem":"To facilitate or ease learning about the autonomic nervous system (or most other things), it’s sometimes helpful to identify a generally applicable “rule” and then learn the one or two main exceptions to it. So, sympathetic innervation of which of the following structures is the exception to the gen- eral rule that “all postganglionic sympathetic nerves are adrenergic?”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arterioles in the skin"},{"label":"B","text":"Arterioles in the viscera"},{"label":"C","text":"Radial muscle in the iris of the eye"},{"label":"D","text":"Sinoatrial node of the heart"},{"label":"E","text":"Sweat glands"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b03bd153c286","stem":"A patient with a history of asthma experiences significant bron- choconstriction and urticaria, and drug-induced histamine release is a main contributor to these responses. Which of the following drugs is most likely to have caused these problems—not because it has any bronchocon- strictor or histamine agonist effects in its own right, but because it quite effectively releases histamine from mast cells?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"C","text":"Pancuronium"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"d-Tubocurarine The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 57"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d1c7573ae8e2","stem":"A patient receives a single injection of succinylcholine to facilitate pre- operative intubation. The dose is correct for the vast majority of patients, and normally effects of this drug abate spontaneously over a couple of min- utes. This gentleman remains apneic for an extraordinarily long time. A genetically based aberrant cholinesterase is eventually determined to be the cause. Which of the following would we administer if we were concerned about this unusually lengthy drug response?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Bethanechol"},{"label":"C","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Nothing"},{"label":"E","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"F","text":"Tubocurarine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f39bc8decd09","stem":"We administer an “effective” dose of a drug and observe the following responses: Stimulates the heart Dilates some blood vessels but constricts none Dilates the bronchi Raises blood glucose levels Neither dilates nor constricts the pupil of the eye Which of the following drugs most likely caused these responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-207175b4389b","stem":"“First-generation” (older) histamine H1 blockers such as diphenhy- dramine, phenothiazine antipsychotic drugs (e.g., chlorpromazine), and tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., imipramine) have pharmacologic actions, side effects, toxicities, and contraindications that are very similar to those of which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antipsychotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Bethanechol"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Propranolol 58 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-615ee0c85d4c","stem":"Physostigmine is the antidote for poisoning with antimuscarinic drugs (e.g., atropine). Another AChE inhibitor, neostigmine, is not suitable. That is because neostigmine cannot overcome the adverse effects of the antimus- carinic drug in or on which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central nervous system (e.g., the brain)"},{"label":"B","text":"Exocrine glands"},{"label":"C","text":"Heart"},{"label":"D","text":"Skeletal muscle"},{"label":"E","text":"Smooth muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c93c4c4509cc","stem":"A patient presents with food poisoning that is attributed to botulism (Botulinus toxin poisoning). Which of the following is a correct character- istic, finding, or mechanism associated with this toxin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete failure of all cholinergic neurotransmission"},{"label":"B","text":"Favorable response to administration of pralidoxime"},{"label":"C","text":"Impairment of parasympathetic, but not sympathetic, nervous system activation"},{"label":"D","text":"Massive overstimulation of all structures having muscarinic cholinergic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Selective paralysis of skeletal muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5097a4ee50dc","stem":"In between your M1 and M2 years you are volunteering in a hospital in a very poor part of the world. Their drug selection is limited. A patient presents with acute cardiac failure, for which your preferred drug is dobu- tamine, given intravenously. However, there is none available. Which of the following other drugs, or combination of drugs, would be a suitable alter- native, giving the pharmacologic equivalent of what you want the dobuta- mine to do? (All these drugs are available in parenteral formulations.)","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopamine (at a very high dose)"},{"label":"B","text":"Ephedrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Ephedrine plus propranolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Norepinephrine plus phentolamine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine plus atropine The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 59"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-5c860e3a9e60","stem":"In general, structures that are affected by sympathetic activation respond to both sympathetic neural activation and to the hormonal com- ponent, epinephrine released from the adrenal medulla. Which of the fol- lowing structures/functions is unique in that it responds to epinephrine, but not norepinephrine, and has no direct neural control?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Airway (tracheal, bronchiolar) smooth muscle: relaxation"},{"label":"B","text":"Atrioventricular node: increased automaticity and conduction velocity"},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary arteries: constriction"},{"label":"D","text":"Iris of the eye: dilation (mydriasis)"},{"label":"E","text":"Renal juxtaglomerular apparatus: renin release"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-990ee76aa8a9","stem":"The figure below shows several responses measured in a subject (healthy; receiving no other drugs) at rest (before) and after receiving a dose of an unknown drug. Note: The blood pressures shown can be con- sidered mean blood pressures; the fall caused by the unknown was mainly due to a fall of diastolic pressure. Which of the following drugs most likely caused the observed responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"C","text":"Neostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Propranolol 60 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9a78189e6099","stem":"A patient received a single dose of succinylcholine for preoperative intubation. Skeletal muscle paralysis during a 3-hr surgery is maintained by a long-acting nondepolarizing type (i.e., curare-like) neuromuscular blocker. Surgery is over and the plan is to reverse the skeletal muscle paral- ysis. Which drug is administered first to block unwanted effects of the reversing agent on smooth muscles and glands, and then which drug is used to actually reverse the skeletal muscle paralysis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine to control smooth muscle, cardiac, and gland responses, then neostig- mine to reverse skeletal muscle paralysis"},{"label":"B","text":"β Blocker first to control cardiac responses, physostigmine for reversal"},{"label":"C","text":"Belladonna alkaloids to block smooth muscle, cardiac, and gland responses, then pralidoxime to restore skeletal muscle function"},{"label":"D","text":"Epinephrine first to control smooth muscle and glands, acetylcholine to reacti- vate skeletal muscle"},{"label":"E","text":"Physostigmine to control smooth muscle and exocrine gland responses, fol- lowed by pancuronium for reversal of skeletal muscle paralysis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6651fa85a007","stem":"You are doing a summer research project that addresses biochemical mechanisms involved in the etiology of pheochromocytomas (most com- monly a tumor of the catecholamine-secreting cells of the adrenal/suprarenal medulla). You want to assess activity of the rate-limiting enzymatic step in cat- echolamine synthesis, and drug effects thereupon. On which of the following enzymes should you focus your studies?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)"},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine decarboxylase"},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine β-hydroxylase"},{"label":"D","text":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltrasnferase"},{"label":"F","text":"Tyrosine hydroxylase The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 61"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3ab69dc6e490","stem":"Adrenergic nerves to the heart are activated, leading to a reflex increase of heart rate and cardiac contractility, in response to a sudden and significant fall of blood pressure. Those sympathetic nerves release norepi- nephrine (NE). Which of the following is the main physiologic mechanism by which the actions of the released NE are terminated?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffusion away from postsynaptic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrolysis by nonspecific deaminases"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic inactivation by MAO"},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic inactivation by catechol-O-methyltransferase"},{"label":"E","text":"Reuptake into the adrenergic nerve from which the NE was released"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-271684cf3e12","stem":"A 59-year-old man has a history of emphysema from 20 years of ciga- rette smoking; hypercholesterolemia that is being managed with atorvas- tatin; and Stage 2 essential hypertension for which he is taking metolazone. He presents in clinic today with his main new complaints: nocturia, uri- nary frequency, and an inability to urinate forcefully and empty his bladder. Following a complete workup, the MD arrives at a diagnosis of benign pro- static hypertrophy (BPH). We start daily therapy with tamsulosin. Which of the following is the most likely side effect the patient may experience from the tamsulosin, and about which he should be forewarned?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bradycardia"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased risk of statin-induced skeletal muscle pathology"},{"label":"C","text":"Orthostatic hypotension"},{"label":"D","text":"Photophobia and other painful responses to bright lights"},{"label":"E","text":"Wheezing or other exacerbations of the emphysema"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d12b67cc291","stem":"A patient walks out of the ophthalmologist’s office and into bright sun- light after a comprehensive eye exam, for which he received a drug, applied topically. The drug has not only dilated his pupils but also impaired his ability to focus his eyes up close. The drug this patient received was most likely classified as, or worked most similar to, which of the following prototypes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Homatropine"},{"label":"D","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine"},{"label":"F","text":"Propranolol 62 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-de756928df0f","stem":"We’ve received approval from the Institutional Review Board to study the in vitro (tissue bath) responsiveness of isolated human arteriolar seg- ments (obtained during surgery) to a variety of pharmacologic and other interventions. The tissue samples are 1-cm-long “cylinders” of otherwise normal (but now denervated) arterioles obtained from the lower legs of patients undergoing amputation surgery for cancer. Our set-up allows us to perfuse the vessels with a solution that will keep the tissue functionally and structurally intact for many hours; to mon- itor and change perfusion pressure (mm Hg; analogous to blood pressure in the intact organism) and perfusate flow (mL/min); and to assess the effects of various vasoactive drugs on the system. We add to the perfusate a concentration of ACh identical to the serum concentration of ACh that causes “expected responses” in an intact human. Under this experimental setup, adding ACh causes a rise of perfusion pressure and a decrease of flow, both of which basically reflect vasocon- striction. Which of the following is the best explanation for these findings?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACh released norepinephrine from the endothelium, which caused vasocon- striction"},{"label":"B","text":"Atropine was added to the tissue bath before adding the ACh"},{"label":"C","text":"Botulinum toxin was added to the bath before adding the ACh"},{"label":"D","text":"The vascular endothelium has been damaged or removed (denuded)"},{"label":"E","text":"This response is precisely what we’d expect with injection of ACh into the intact human"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4cd49223d17","stem":"A 33-year-old woman becomes poisoned after receiving an injection of illicitly prepared and overly concentrated botulinum toxin. Which of the following is the main neurochemical mechanism by which this Clostridium toxin causes its effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Directly activates all muscarinic and nicotinic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits ACh release from all cholinergic nerves"},{"label":"C","text":"Prevents neuronal norepinephrine reuptake"},{"label":"D","text":"Releases norepinephrine via a nonexocytotic process"},{"label":"E","text":"Selectively and competitively blocks nicotinic receptors The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 63"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-605eceb0d0a1","stem":"A patient takes a massive overdose of diphenhydramine, suffering not only significant CNS depression but also numerous and serious peripheral autonomic side effects. Which of the following is the main mechanism by which diphenhydramine exerts its untoward peripheral autonomic actions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of both β1 and β2 adrenoceptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockade of α-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Competitive antagonism of ACh actions on muscarinic receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Massive, direct overactivation of ganglionic nicotinic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Sudden release of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla (suprarenal medulla)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d9a503606f1","stem":"A patient with a recent drug poisoning is transported to the emergency department. The physician orders (correctly, in this case) administration of pralidoxime as part of the comprehensive emergency treatment plan. Which one of the following best describes who the patient was?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 13-year-old boy who took an overdose of methylphenidate for his ADD/ADHD"},{"label":"B","text":"A 43-year-old who took an overdose of neostigmine, prescribed for her myas- thenia gravis, in a suicide attempt"},{"label":"C","text":"A 6-year-old who got into the family medicine cabinet and took 10 “adult doses” of her dad’s prazosin"},{"label":"D","text":"A farm/field worker accidentally doused with insecticide from an overflying crop-duster plane"},{"label":"E","text":"An asthma patient who accidentally gave himself an intravenous injection of epinephrine in an attempt to self-treat a developing anaphylactic reaction"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da6b366c0ef3","stem":"To facilitate a certain eye exam we want to cause mydriasis, but not alter normal control of accommodation. All of the following drugs are available as topical ophthalmic formulations. Which of the following would achieve our goals the best?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Homatropine"},{"label":"D","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine"},{"label":"F","text":"Timolol 64 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-94fd04b09347","stem":"A 26-year-old woman has rhinorrhea, excessive lacrimation, and ocu- lar congestion from a bout with the common cold. Diphenhydramine pro- vides symptomatic relief. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism by which this drug gave symptom relief in the presence of this rhinovirus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-Adrenergic activation (agonist)"},{"label":"B","text":"β-Adrenergic blockade"},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium channel blockade"},{"label":"D","text":"Histamine (H1) receptor blockade"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscarinic receptor blockade"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-56f145ff0ba6","stem":"You have treated dozens of patients with acute hypotension from var- ious causes, including overdoses of antihypertensive drugs, all of which can cause hypotension. Your usual approach to restoring blood pressure, and one that has worked well every time before, is to inject x mg of phenylephrine intravenously. Today a patient with severe drug-induced hypotension presents in the emergency department. He has been taking this drug for many months. He is not volume-depleted, nor hemorrhaging. You give the phenylephrine at the same dose and by the same route as you always have. It causes no change of blood pressure. Which of the fol- lowing drugs did the patient most likely take an overdose of?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atenolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Bethanechol"},{"label":"C","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Reserpine The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic 65"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-7104d5d0750a","stem":"This is a strange day for you in the emergency department. Now you have to treat another normovolemic patient with acute drug-induced hypotension, and give the usually correct and effective dose of phenyle- phrine. This time the drug causes a vasopressor response that is far greater than you’ve ever encountered: systolic pressure rises dramatically, if not dangerously. Which of the following drugs did the patient most likely take an over- dose of?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atenolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Bethanechol"},{"label":"C","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Reserpine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1eee5217186d","stem":"A 43-year-old woman with diagnosed myasthenia gravis presents with profound skeletal muscle weakness. We are unsure whether she is experi- encing a cholinergic crisis or a myasthenic crisis, so we administer a usu- ally appropriate dose of parenteral edrophonium. Assume the patient was actually experiencing a cholinergic crisis. Which of the following is the most likely response to the drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensive crisis from peripheral vascular constriction"},{"label":"B","text":"Myocardial ischemia, and angina, from drug-induced tachycardia and coronary vasoconstriction"},{"label":"C","text":"Premature ventricular contractions from increased ventricular automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"Prompt improvement of skeletal muscle tone and function"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventilatory distress or failure The Peripheral Nervous Systems: Autonomic and Somatic Answers"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ea92c344aae8","stem":"The answer is g. (Brunton, pp 139–141; Craig, p 93; Katzung, pp 76, 84–86.) The neurotransmitter released from nerve d, the postganglionic sympathetic fibers (to structures other than most sweat glands), is norepi- nephrine. NE can activate α-adrenergic receptors (both α1 and α2), and β1 receptors (not β2). Of course, different structures have different subtypes of adrenergic receptors: such structures as arterioles and the iris dilator mus- cle have α1 receptors; β1s are found in the heart, while β2 receptors (not activated by NE) are found on various smooth muscles in the GI and uri- nary tracts and airways. So, the only correct response to the question “which receptors are activated?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3cc40cf4ec85","stem":"The answer is h. (Brunton, pp 220–225; Craig, pp 338–340, 342–344; Katzung, pp 75–76.) Pancuronium and tubocurarine (as well as metocurine and several related drugs) are nondepolarizing skeletal neuromuscular blockers (quite differently, mechanistically, from succinylcholine, a depo- larizing blocker). They specifically and competitively block activation of nicotinic receptors on skeletal muscle by ACh, thereby preventing depolar- ization of skeletal muscle; and have no effect on muscarinic receptors or any of the adrenergic receptor subtypes. (Remember something about the natives of the Amazon obtaining a poisonous substance from the skin of certain species of frogs, putting it on the tips of their darts, and using that to kill their dinner with a blowgun?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d0d26470ad21","stem":"The answer is a. (Brunton, pp 166t, 189–195, 197; Craig, pp 134–139; Katzung, pp 116, 116t.) You may never have learned about oxybutynin (although you’ve probably seen an ad for its brand-name product on TV). But it makes no difference to answering this rather easy question. The description of this unusual drug is, in reality, also an excellent description of many of the properties of atropine, the prototype muscarinic receptor blocking drug. You should also be able to eliminate incorrect answers by a simple process of elimination. For example, do β blockers (b) cause the visual, uri- nary tract, sweat gland, or cardiovascular responses we noted?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f2e9693d9888","stem":") Ritodrine is a selective β2-adrenergic agonist that relaxes uterine smooth muscle. It also has the other effects attributable to β-adrenergic receptor agonists, such as bronchodilation, cardiac stimulation, enhanced renin secretion, and hyperglycemia. Yes, it is classified the same as, for example, albuterol, salmeterol, or terbutaline, and several other drugs typi- cally used as adrenergic bronchodilators. But ridodrine is approved for use as a tocolytic (uterine-relaxing) drug. Will it, or can it, cause bronchodila- tion?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-904291fafe5c","stem":"The answer is b. (Brunton, pp 163–164; Craig, pp 90–92; Katzung, pp 81–83.) Norepinephrine (and other monoamine neurotransmitters such as dopamine) is removed from its receptors by an “amine pump” located in the neuronal membrane. Recall that this reuptake process can be blocked by (among other drugs) cocaine and tricyclic antidepressants. (You might ask “what about ‘stimulation’ of presynaptic α2 receptors?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["possible_web_capture_contamination"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["possible_web_capture_contamination"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f457a38c4f3d","stem":"The answer is a. (Brunton, pp 23–25, 272, 274t, 276, 282t; Craig, pp 113–114; Katzung, pp 150, 153–154.) The β blockers with intrinsic sympa- thomimetic activity (ISA) are partial agonists—they act simultaneously as both β agonists and competitive β blockers. How can that be?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ad394baffb8e","stem":"The answer is c. (Brunton, pp 143t–144t, 250; Craig, pp 93, 102; Katzung, pp 128–131.) Piece things together. Some tip-offs to help arrive at the that has effects on the “parasympathetic” side, whether as an agonist or The Peripheral Nervous Systems:Autonomic and Somatic Answers 91 antagonist (here, atropine), and rule out any drug with α effects. (As noted above, no β receptors control the size of the pupil of the eye.) Does isoproterenol fit all the other criteria/properties? Yes. And none of the other choices have all the stated properties, only some of them.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-bc936f486f3e","stem":"The answer is b. (Brunton, pp 143t–145t, 234t; Craig, p 93; Katzung, pp 86, 129–131.) To me, the tip-off that helps get to the correct answer, iso- proterenol, is to focus (no pun intended) on the fact that the unknown drug did not change the size of the pupil of the eye. Of all the main autonomic receptor types, adrenergic and cholinergic, only the β receptors play no role in regulating the size of the pupil. That narrows things down to isopro- terenol or propranolol. Propranolol might lower BP (particularly in a hyper- tensive patient), but it would not raise heart rate or dilate the bronchi. Only isoproterenol fits the bill. And the other answers?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c41106ffaa79","stem":"The answer is c. (Brunton, pp 254, 260, 263–267, 269–271; Craig, pp 105, 111–113; Katzung, pp 122–124, 130–131, 142–145.) Phenylephrine raises blood pressure by activating α-adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth- muscle cells. Prazosin competitively blocks those receptors, and when present at sufficiently high doses (as might occur with an acute, severe over- dose) may eliminate the vasopressor response to even higher than usual doses of phenylephrine. β Blockers (atenolol, propranolol) have no α-block- ing activity and so won’t reduce the vasopressor response to phenylephrine. Nor will bethanechol, which causes vasodilation by activating muscarinic receptors for ACh. And reserpine?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9efbfcbae203","stem":"A 42-year-old woman develops akathisias, parkinsonian-like dyski- nesias, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea, during drug therapy. Which of the following drug-receptor-based mechanisms, occurring in the central ner- vous system, most likely caused these responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blockade of α-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockade of dopamine receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Blockade of muscarinic receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Supersensitivity of dopamine receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of nicotinic receptors 103 Copyright © 2007 by The McGraw-Hill Companies, Inc. Click here for terms of use. 104 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["source_attribution_in_option"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fa82d67b08f6","stem":"A patient on the trauma-burn unit receives a drug to ease the pain of debridement and dressing changes. The patient experiences good, prompt analgesia, but despite the absence of pain sensation during the procedure her heart rate and blood pressure rise considerably, consistent with the concept that the sympathetic nervous system was activated by the pain and not affected by the analgesic drug. As the effects of the drug develop, the patient’s skeletal muscle tone progressively increases. The patient appears awake at times because the eyes periodically open. As drug effects wear off, the patient hallucinates and behaves in a very agitated fashion. Which of the following drug was most likely given?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fentanyl"},{"label":"B","text":"Ketamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Midazolam"},{"label":"D","text":"Succinylcholine"},{"label":"E","text":"Thiopental"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82ee5aa5cca8","stem":"A patient who has been treated with levodopa is switched to a regimen with a proprietary product that contains both levodopa and carbidopa. Which of the following is the main action of carbidopa that provides the rationale for using it in this combined approach?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blocks ACh release in the CNS, thereby facilitating levodopa’s ability to restore a dopamine-ACh balance"},{"label":"B","text":"Helps activate dietary vitamin B6, a deficiency of which occurs during levodopa therapy"},{"label":"C","text":"Increases permeability of the blood-brain barrier to levodopa, giving levodopa better access to the CNS"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits metabolic conversion of levodopa to dopamine outside the CNS"},{"label":"E","text":"Reduces levodopa-induced hypotension by blocking vascular dopamine receptors The Central Nervous System 105"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-32573e2e4e6f","stem":"The attending with whom you are working in the emergency depart- ment quizzes you on your basic knowledge of local anesthetics and their uses. She asks you to identify the drug that is suitable for topical adminis- tration (e.g., to mucous membranes), but cannot be given parenterally because of its physicochemical properties, which render it very poorly sol- uble. Which of the following drugs is the best answer to her question?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benzocaine"},{"label":"B","text":"Bupivacaine"},{"label":"C","text":"Etidocaine"},{"label":"D","text":"Mepivacaine"},{"label":"E","text":"Procaine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b09eb6858460","stem":"A physician considers placing a patient on long-term (months, years) phenobarbital for control of a relatively common medical condition. For most of these indications, newer and arguably more efficacious drugs are available and preferred. For which of the following, however, is it still con- sidered reasonable and appropriate to use this barbiturate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcohol withdrawal signs/symptoms"},{"label":"B","text":"Anxiety management"},{"label":"C","text":"Certain epilepsies"},{"label":"D","text":"Endogenous depression (adjunct to SSRIs)"},{"label":"E","text":"Sleep disorders such as insomnia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-33415e65294a","stem":"One reason for the declining use of tricyclic antidepressants such as imipramine, and the growing use of newer classes, is the prevalence of common tricyclic-induced side effects or adverse responses. Which of the following best describes the side effects or adverse responses that are most likely to occur with the administration of a tricyclic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) effects"},{"label":"B","text":"Arrhythmias"},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatotoxicity"},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrotoxicity"},{"label":"E","text":"Seizures 106 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c7dc4ad55578","stem":"A 17-year-old male was diagnosed with epilepsy after developing repeated episodes of generalized tonic-clonic sezures following a motor vehi- cle accident in which he received a closed head injury. After treating acute seizures with the proper injectable drugs, he is started on a regimen of oral phenytoin, the daily dose titrated upwards until symptom control and a ther- apeutic plasma concentration were reached. The elimination half-life of the drug during initial treatment was measured to be 24 h, a value that is quite typical for otherwise healthy adults taking no other drugs. Today he presents in the neurology clinic with nystagmus, ataxia, diplopia, cognitive impairment, and other signs and symptoms consistent with phenytoin toxicity. A blood sample, drawn at noon, has a plasma phenytoin concentration of 30 mcg/mL. That value is 50% higher than typ- ical peak therapeutic serum concentrations and twice the usual minimum effective blood level. These values are summarized in the figure. We will withhold further doses of phenytoin until plasma levels fall into the therapeutic range, and the patient is largely free of signs and symp- toms of phenytoin toxicity. However, which of the following statements correctly summarizes what we should do, or expect to occur, next?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer flumazenil, which will quickly reverse signs and symptoms of phenytoin toxicity but may cause seizures to recur"},{"label":"B","text":"Elimination of phenytoin from the plasma will follow zero-order kinetics for several days"},{"label":"C","text":"Give an amphetamine or other CNS stimulant to reverse generalized CNS depression due to the phenytoin excess"},{"label":"D","text":"Give phenobarbital to induce the P450 system, thereby hastening phenytoin’s metabolic elimination"},{"label":"E","text":"Plasma phenytoin concentrations will fall to 15 mcg/mL, in the middle of the therapeutic range, by noon the next day (24 h later, per the usual half-life) The Central Nervous System 107"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4e456cbcf41d","stem":"We have a patient with Parkinson’s disease. Signs and symptoms can be classified as “moderate” now but progressive and not responding well to cur- rent drug therapy. The physician decides to empirically assess an antiparkin- son drug that is a selective inhibitor of monoamine oxidase type B (MAO-B). Which of the following drugs would that be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"B","text":"Carbidopa"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenelzine"},{"label":"D","text":"Selegiline"},{"label":"E","text":"Tranylcypromine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9061e46aa060","stem":"Meperidine is similar to morphine in many ways. However, with very high blood levels or with true overdoses, meperidine can cause significant adverse responses that simply aren’t seen with morphine or most other opioid analgesics. Which of the following best identifies that rather unique effect of meperidine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constipation leading to paralytic ileus"},{"label":"B","text":"Heightened response to pain (paradoxical hyperalgesia)"},{"label":"C","text":"Intense biliary tract spasm"},{"label":"D","text":"Psychosis-like state, possibly seizures"},{"label":"E","text":"Respiratory depression, apnea, ventilatory arrest"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f730e1141b4c","stem":"Chlorpromazine and haloperidol can be considered prototypes of two relatively old but still used antipsychotic drugs—the phenothiazines and the butyrophenones—respectively. While many of the actions and side effects of these drugs are qualitatively similar, they are different quantitatively: that is, in terms of incidence and severity. Which of the following effects or side effects typically occur more frequently, and could be said to be more severe, with haloperidol?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antipsychotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extrapyramidal reactions"},{"label":"B","text":"Intense atropine-like side effects"},{"label":"C","text":"Lethal blood dyscrasias"},{"label":"D","text":"Orthostatic hypotension"},{"label":"E","text":"Urinary retention necessitating bladder catheterization 108 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b015525997bc","stem":"A patient is transported to the emergency department. A friend who accompanies the patient to the ED says “he was experimenting with (PCP phencyclidine).” Which of the following best describes the actions of phen- cyclidine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes its peripheral and central effects via antimuscarinic properties"},{"label":"B","text":"Causes significant withdrawal symptoms"},{"label":"C","text":"Has hallucinogenic properties"},{"label":"D","text":"Has strong opioid receptor-activating activity"},{"label":"E","text":"Overdoses should be treated with flumazenil"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-95dc292d33e9","stem":"After a few weeks on a drug, a patient reports profound thirst and the production of copious volumes of clear (dilute) urine each day. Which of the following drugs is most likely responsible for the signs and symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"B","text":"Fluoxetine"},{"label":"C","text":"Haloperidol"},{"label":"D","text":"Lithium"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be5fbb687ff6","stem":"A 12-year-old boy has been treated with methylphenidate for the last 3 years. His younger sister finds the bottle of pills and consumes enough to cause significant toxicity. Which of the following findings would you most likely expect?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertension, tachycardia, seizures"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotension, bronchospasm"},{"label":"C","text":"Drowsiness, obtunded reflexes, diarrhea"},{"label":"D","text":"Miosis, bradycardia, profuse salivation, sweating"},{"label":"E","text":"Hypothermia, skeletal muscle weakness or paralysis, pupils that are not respon- sive to light The Central Nervous System 109"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c0ecf675843d","stem":"Two inhaled general anesthetics, A and B, have the following MAC values: true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"MAC = 2%"},{"label":"B","text":"MAC = 100% Based on this information alone, which of the following statements is"},{"label":"A","text":"Drug A has a longer duration of action than Drug B"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug A is more soluble in the blood than Drug B"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug B causes greater analgesia and skeletal muscle relaxation than Drug A"},{"label":"D","text":"The concentration of drug in inspired air that is needed to cause adequate sur- gical anesthesia is higher for Drug B than for Drug A"},{"label":"E","text":"The time to onset of adequate general anesthesia is 50 times longer for Drug B than for Drug A"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-778ac935b171","stem":"A 31-year-old woman has been treated with fluoxetine for 5 months. She is diagnosed with another medical problem and receives one or more drugs that, otherwise, would be suitable and probably problem-free. She is rushed to the ED with unstable vital signs, muscle rigidity, myoclonus, CNS irritability and altered consciousness, and shivering. Which of the fol- lowing add-on drugs most likely causes these responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Codeine for cough"},{"label":"B","text":"Loratadine for seasonal allergies"},{"label":"C","text":"Midazolam and fentanyl, used to ease discomfort from endoscopy"},{"label":"D","text":"Sumatriptan for migraine"},{"label":"E","text":"Zolpidem for short-term insomnia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6e055be434f3","stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a long history of anxiety that has been treated with diazepam decides to triple her daily dose because of increas- ing fearfulness about “environmental noises.” Two days after her attempt at self-prescribing, she is found extremely lethargic and nonresponsive, with markedly obtunded reflexes and reaction to painful stimuli. Respirations are 8/min and shallow. Which of the following drugs should we give specif- ically to reverse these signs and symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dextroamphetamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"C","text":"Naltrexone"},{"label":"D","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Pralidoxime 110 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e3e690358f6a","stem":"A patient who has been treated for Parkinson’s disease for about a year presents with purplish, mottled changes to her skin. Which of the fol- lowing drugs is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amantadine"},{"label":"B","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"C","text":"Levodopa (alone)"},{"label":"D","text":"Levodopa combined with carbidopa"},{"label":"E","text":"Pramipexole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-697fb08c5ac7","stem":"A young boy who has been treated for epilepsy for a year is referred to a periodontist for evaluation and probable treatment of massive over- growth of his gingival tissues. Some teeth are almost completely covered with hyperplastic tissue. Which of the following drugs was the most likely cause of the oral pathology?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamazepine"},{"label":"B","text":"Lorazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"E","text":"Valproic acid"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39f1fb8bf784","stem":"A patient with undiagnosed coronary artery disease is given a med- ication. Shortly thereafter she develops intense tightness and “crushing dis- comfort” of her chest. An EKG reveals ST–segment changes indicative of acute myocardial ischemia. The patient suffered acute myocardial ischemia and angina pectoris as a result of the drug. Which of the following drugs most likely causes this reaction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clozapine"},{"label":"B","text":"Pentazocine"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"D","text":"Sumatriptan"},{"label":"E","text":"Zolpidem The Central Nervous System 111"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3233b6c6b28d","stem":"A patient with status epilepticus has received an IV dose of lorazepam. At the institution at which you trained, phenytoin would be given next, but the attending orders fosphenytoin. Which of the following best describes the main advantage of the fosphenytoin over phenytoin itself?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes less vascular/venous irritation, can be injected at a faster rate"},{"label":"B","text":"Directly stimulates ventilation (action in brain’s medulla) that is compromised during status epilepticus"},{"label":"C","text":"Has a mechanism of anticonvulsant action that is different from and more effec- tive than plain phenytoin"},{"label":"D","text":"Has such a long duration of action that oral anticonvulsant therapy is not needed once the status epilepticus has been stopped"},{"label":"E","text":"Lacks intrinsic anticonvulsant activity but slows lorazepam clearance, thereby prolonging lorazepam’s anticonvulsant action"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ac991cf8de81","stem":"Nitrous oxide is a common component in the technique of balanced anesthesia. It is used in conjunction with such other drugs as a halogenated hydrocarbon volatile liquid anesthetic, and usually included as 80% of the total inspired gas mixture. Which of the following best summarizes why nitrous oxide cannot be used alone for general anesthesia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Almost total lack of analgesic activity, regardless of concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"Inspired concentrations > 10% tend to profound cardiac negative inotropic effects"},{"label":"C","text":"MAC (minimum alveolar concentration) is > 100%"},{"label":"D","text":"Methemoglobinemia occurs even with low inspired concentrations"},{"label":"E","text":"Such great solubility in blood that its effects take an extraordinarily long time to develop"},{"label":"F","text":"Very high frequency of bronchospasm"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e1f65a8dd25e","stem":"A patient develops a severe and rapidly worsening adverse response to a drug. The physician orders prompt administration of antipyretics, IV hydration, and bromocriptine or dantrolene to manage symptoms and hopefully to prevent a fatal outcome. Which of the following drugs or drug groups most likely causes these adverse responses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benzodiazepines, especially those used as hypnotics"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpromazine"},{"label":"C","text":"Levodopa"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"E","text":"SSRIs 112 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f3ac55906f2f","stem":"Ropinirole is a relatively new drug that recently was approved to treat what’s commonly called restless legs syndrome (also known as Ekbom syn- drome). The drug works as a dopamine receptor agonist in certain parts of the brain. Given this mechanism of action, which of the following other disorders is most likely another indication for this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Daytime anxiety"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypersomnia (excessive sleepiness)"},{"label":"C","text":"Parkinson’s disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Schizophrenia"},{"label":"E","text":"Status epilepticus"},{"label":"F","text":"Treatment of severe pain"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-691167382cae","stem":"A 34-year-old man with mild anxiety and depression symptoms has heard about buspirone on television and asks whether it might be suitable for him. According to the latest diagnostic criteria, the drug would be appropri- ate, particularly for short-term symptom control. Which of the following best describes an important property of this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with a withdrawal syndrome that, if unsupervised, is frequently lethal"},{"label":"B","text":"Has a significant potential for abuse"},{"label":"C","text":"Is likely to potentiate the CNS depressant effects of alcohol, benzodiazepines, and sedative antihistamines (e.g., diphenhydramine), so such interactants must be avoided at all cost"},{"label":"D","text":"Requires almost daily dosage titrations in order to optimize the response"},{"label":"E","text":"Seldom causes drowsiness"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5485eec51aa","stem":"A patient in the neurology unit at your hospital develops status epilepticus. Which of the following is the best first IV drug to give?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamazepine"},{"label":"B","text":"Lorazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"E","text":"Valproic acid The Central Nervous System 113"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ac62985a2cf5","stem":"A patient has had a documented severe allergic reaction to ester-type local anesthetics. Which one of the following is also a member of the ester class, and so would be the most likely to provoke an allergic or anaphylac- tic reaction if this patient received it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupivicaine"},{"label":"B","text":"Lidocaine"},{"label":"C","text":"Mepivacaine"},{"label":"D","text":"Prilocaine"},{"label":"E","text":"Tetracaine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0a1f27dae912","stem":"A 66-year-old woman is diagnosed with Alzheimer’s disease, with symptoms being described as mild-to-moderate. Which of the following pharmacologic approaches best summarizes a mechanism of action by which current drugs, used for early Alzheimer’s and having actions mainly in the CNS, provide some slowing of the underlying brain pathology?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activate a population of serotonin receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Block dopamine release or receptor activation"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibit acetylcholinesterase"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibit MAO"},{"label":"E","text":"Dissolve cerebral vascular thrombi"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6593328c7b08","stem":"Trihexyphenidyl is prescribed as an adjunct to other drugs being used to manage a patient with Parkinson’s disease. Which of the following is the most likely purpose or action of this drug as part of the overall drug treatment plan?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"To counteract sedation that is likely to be caused by the other medications"},{"label":"B","text":"To help correct further the dopamine-ACh imbalance that accounts for parkin- sonian signs and symptoms"},{"label":"C","text":"To manage cutaneous allergic responses that are so common with “typical” antiparkinson drugs"},{"label":"D","text":"To prevent the development of manic/hypomanic responses to other antiparkin- son drugs"},{"label":"E","text":"To reverse tardive dyskinesias if the parkinsonism was induced by an antipsy- chotic drug 114 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-0be8a195e31a","stem":"In early 2006 the FDA granted approval to market a new prescrip- tion drug (“Drug X”) that will be administered in the form of a dermal patch (apply the patch to intact skin, the drug is absorbed from there). This new drug belongs to a very old class of drugs that, when given by its usual route—orally—can interact with foods such as cheese and processed meats (and certain breads, other foods, and alcoholic beverages) leading to an interaction that can elevate blood pressure (to severe and sometimes fatal lev- els). After more than a decade of testing, the FDA approved its use for adults. In its lowest dose, no dietary restrictions are required. ” Based on this information, how is this new drug, Drug X, most likely classified, and which of the following is its most likely clinical use?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphetamine-like agent for ADD/ADHD"},{"label":"B","text":"Barbiturates used for daytime anxiety"},{"label":"C","text":"Benzodiazepine for anxiety and sleep"},{"label":"D","text":"MAO inhibitor for depression"},{"label":"E","text":"Morphine-like analgesic for severe/chronic pain"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-57f612248fc5","stem":"The pediatrician writes a prescription for a combination (of several drugs) product that contains dextromethorphan, which is an isomer of a codeine analog. The patient is a 12-year-old boy. Which of the following is the most likely purpose for which the drug was prescribed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Control mild-moderate pain after the lad broke his wrist playing soccer"},{"label":"B","text":"Manage diarrhea caused by food-borne bacteria"},{"label":"C","text":"Provide sedation because the child has ADD/ADHD"},{"label":"D","text":"Suppress severe cough associated with a bout of influenza"},{"label":"E","text":"Treat nocturnal bed-wetting"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82f83d299662","stem":"We administer a “usually effective” and otherwise therapeutic dose of thiopental to a patient. It is given by IV bolus injection. Within a matter of seconds the patient is asleep. We give no other drug. Which of the follow- ing is most likely to occur thereafter?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Significant and clinically useful analgesia will persist for an hour or so"},{"label":"B","text":"Significant increase in cerebral oxygen consumption"},{"label":"C","text":"The drug will be promptly metabolized by the hepatic P450 system"},{"label":"D","text":"The patient develops acute seizures (e.g., status epilepticus)"},{"label":"E","text":"The patient will awake in about 3–5 min The Central Nervous System 115"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2ba17252682c","stem":"Many legal jurisdictions have imposed various restrictions on over- the-counter sale of products that contain pseudoephedrine, for example, various oral decongestant products. That is because pseudoephedrine can be rather easily used to synthesize which of the following highly psychoac- tive and abuse-prone drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methamphetamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Morphine"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxycodone"},{"label":"D","text":"Pentazocine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phencyclidine (“PCP”)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f2c7f155ef48","stem":"The anesthesiologist prepares to administer several drugs to a patient as part of normal pre- and intraoperative care. Which of the following lacks, as its normal spectrum of action, a direct ability either to suppress overall CNS function or the patient’s level of consciousness, or to provide analgesia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clonidine"},{"label":"B","text":"Droperidol"},{"label":"C","text":"Pancuronium"},{"label":"D","text":"Propofol"},{"label":"E","text":"Thiopental"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-75288626704d","stem":"A 26-year-old woman with depression has been on antidepressant therapy for several months. Today she complains of missing her period and having galactorrhea, and your careful assessment suggests she has devel- oped some dyskinesias not unlike those you would typically associate with a phenothiazine or butyrophenone (e.g., haloperidol) antipsychotic drug. Pregnancy tests are negative. Which of the following is most likely to have caused these findings?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antidepressant","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxapine"},{"label":"B","text":"Citalopram"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoxetine"},{"label":"D","text":"Sertraline"},{"label":"E","text":"Tranylcypromine 116 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a25dbe17cba7","stem":"A patient has been taking an oral monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI), but that fact is unknown to the health team who is now taking care of her, for unrelated medical conditions, in the hospital. The patient receives a drug that leads to a fatal response characterized by profound fever, delirium, psychotic behavior, and status epilepticus. It was found to have occurred because of an interaction with the MAOI. Which of the following drugs, or class of drugs, was most likely administered by the attending health team?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A barbiturate"},{"label":"B","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Meperidine"},{"label":"E","text":"Morphine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb4d3290322e","stem":"A young woman is taken to the emergency department by some of her friends. It seems they were out on “bar night” and someone slipped some- thing into her alcoholic beverage, the first and only one she consumed that night. She is now extraordinarily drowsy and has little recall of what hap- pened between the time she sipped her drink and now. Someone overheard another bar patron talking about “roofies.” Being knowledgeable about your pharmacology you suspect her drink was spiked with rohypnol. Assuming your guess is correct, which of the following drugs is most likely to reverse the flunitrazepam’s effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"B","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"C","text":"Ketamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Naltrexone"},{"label":"E","text":"Triazolam The Central Nervous System 117"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ae2066e274c7","stem":"In deciding on pharmacotherapy for many patients you’ve diagnosed with depression, you’ve usually considered starting with an SSRI or, in some cases, a tricyclic. Today you assess a patient and suspect endogenous depres- sion. While discussing treatment options they refer to a drug by name and ask you about it; they’ve seen many advertisements for it in magazines and on television. The drug (generic name) is bupropion. Which of the following statements best describes bupropion in terms of how it differs from either or both the SSRIs or tricyclics?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher incidence of CNS depression, drowsiness"},{"label":"B","text":"Higher incidence of weight gain"},{"label":"C","text":"Less drug-induced sexual dysfunction"},{"label":"D","text":"Much more common and severe falls of resting blood pressure and orthostatic hypotension"},{"label":"E","text":"More severe and more frequent peripheral anticholinergic (atropine-like) side effects"},{"label":"F","text":"Stronger inhibition of monoamine oxidase"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4f2b5e80b84","stem":"A 33-year-old woman patient treated with haloperidol is seen in the emergency department (ED). Her husband describes complaints of rapidly worsening fever, muscle stiffness, and tremor. Her level of consciousness is diminishing. Her temperature is 104°F, and her serum creatine kinase (CK) level is elevated. Which of the following is the best explanation for these findings?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allergic response to her medication"},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)"},{"label":"C","text":"Overdose"},{"label":"D","text":"Parkinsonism"},{"label":"E","text":"Tardive dyskinesia 118 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-5108f6a67c77","stem":"A patient will receive bupivacaine, infused continuously via an epidural catheter, for prolonged analgesia during postoperative recovery. Which of the following is the greatest concern when using this drug, com- pared with what one might expect with otherwise equianalgesic doses of the overall local anesthetic prototype, lidocaine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arrhythmias"},{"label":"B","text":"Greater paralysis of skeletal muscle (motor block) than sensory block"},{"label":"C","text":"Higher risk of bronchospasm"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertension, hypertensive crisis"},{"label":"E","text":"Nephrotoxicity"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-08ed5047f29b","stem":"Nearly all the drugs used as primary therapy, or as adjuncts, for the treatment of Parkinson’s disease or drug-induced parkinsonism exert their desired effects directly in the brain’s striatum. Which of the following exerts its main effects in the gut, not in the brain?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amantadine"},{"label":"B","text":"Benztropine"},{"label":"C","text":"Bromcriptine"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbidopa"},{"label":"E","text":"Selegiline"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-47a74c27a8f3","stem":"You have a patient with severe postoperative pain, who is not getting adequate analgesia from usually effective doses of morphine. The physician orders an immediate switch to pentazocine (at usually effective analgesic doses). Which of the following is the most likely outcome of stopping the morphine and immediately substituting the pentazocine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abrupt, added respiratory depression"},{"label":"B","text":"Acute development of physical dependence"},{"label":"C","text":"Coma"},{"label":"D","text":"Seizures"},{"label":"E","text":"Worsening of pain The Central Nervous System 119"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-de41e8c66bab","stem":"Our chosen pharmacologic approach to managing a patient with mild and recently diagnosed parkinsonism will be to enhance specifically the activ- ity of endogenous brain dopamine by inhibiting its metabolic inactivation. Which one of the following drugs works primarily by that mechanism?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benztropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Selegiline"},{"label":"C","text":"Trihexyphenidyl"},{"label":"D","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"E","text":"Chlorpromazine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5668ff851bb","stem":"Chlorpromazine has been prescribed for a patient with schizophre- nia, and the patient has been taking the drug, at usually effective doses, for about 6 months. Today he comes to the hospital with other medical condi- tions that require surgery and the administration of other drugs, and we decide it is unwise to stop the chlorpromazine and run the risk of psychotic behavior while we perform other interventions. Which of the following other signs/symptoms that the patient may also have or acquire as the result of surgery and drug therapy is most likely to be affected beneficially by the continued use of chlorpromazine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epilepsy and the risk of seizures"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotension"},{"label":"C","text":"Nausea and vomiting"},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary retention caused by abdominal surgery"},{"label":"E","text":"Xerostomia (dry mouth) caused by antimuscarinic drugs used to prevent intra- operative bradycardia 120 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1e55cf4f6562","stem":"There are, rightfully, concerns about cocaine abuse, and too many deaths have occurred from smoking “crack” cocaine or injecting or nasally inhaling the drug. Which of the following statements best describes the main mechanism by which cocaine exerts its deleterious effects in the cen- tral nervous system or in the periphery?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Directly activates, as an agonist, both α- and β1-adrenergic receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Enhances neuronally-mediated adrenergic-receptor activation by inhibiting neuronal-norepinephrine reuptake"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits catecholamine inactivation by inhibiting MAO and catechol-O-methyl- transferase"},{"label":"D","text":"Produces bradycardia and vasodilation, leading to hypotension and acute heart failure, by blocking neuronal NE release"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulates autonomic nerve conduction effectively, leading to increased neu- ronal norepinephrine release"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5dab347b2aa","stem":"We administer a drug with a pharmacologic profile consistent with selective activation (as an agonist) of dopamine D2 receptors. Which of the following is the most likely drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpromazine"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluphenazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Haloperidol"},{"label":"E","text":"Promethazine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ed368ceaf32c","stem":"We perform a meta-analysis on the ability of various antipsychotic drugs to cause constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, and dry mouth—all of which reflect significant blockade of muscarinic receptors in the peripheral nervous system. Which of the following drugs, or the main drug class to which it belongs, was most likely to cause these unwanted effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorpromazine"},{"label":"B","text":"Clozapine"},{"label":"C","text":"Haloperidol"},{"label":"D","text":"Olanzapine"},{"label":"E","text":"Sertraline The Central Nervous System 121"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-61c127fe2187","stem":"A patient is on long-term methadone therapy as part of a holistic plan to curb the opioid addiction and abuse. Which of the following best describes a characteristic of this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes pentazocine-like activation of κ receptors and blockade of μ receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Has greater oral bioavailability than morphine, especially when oral adminis- tration is started"},{"label":"C","text":"Remarkably devoid of such typical opioid analgesic side effects as constipation and respiratory depression"},{"label":"D","text":"Useful for maintenance therapy in opioid- (e.g., heroin-) dependent individu- als, but lacks clinically useful analgesic effects"},{"label":"E","text":"When abruptly stopped after long-term administration, causes a withdrawal syndrome that is more intense, but briefer, than that associated with morphine or heroin withdrawal"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-092d0dfd7545","stem":"A 14-year-old girl is brought to the ED by her mother, who has observed that her daughter has abruptly experienced frequent impairments of consciousness associated with episodes of staring into space lasting approximately 30 s. Further neurologic evaluation indicates signs and symptoms consistent with absence seizures. With which of the following drugs should we start treatment?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alprazolam"},{"label":"B","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Ethosuximide"},{"label":"D","text":"Midazolam"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b13174087b0","stem":"A 43-year-old woman becomes hypertensive and suffers a fatal acute coronary syndrome shortly after starting therapy on a drug. Autopsy shows little in the way of coronary atherosclerosis, but EKG changes noted just before her death revealed significant myocardial ischemia in the myocardium served by the left anterior descending and circumflex coro- nary arteries. The cause of death is thought to involve coronary vasospasm. Which of the following drugs most likely precipitated this event?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine for Parkinson’s disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Ergotamine given to abort a migraine attack"},{"label":"C","text":"Morphine for post-trauma analgesia"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenoxybenzamine used for carcinoid syndrome"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin to manage generalized tonic-clonic seizures 122 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e6bbfbc97d70","stem":"A 20-year-old man with absence seizures is treated with ethosuximide. Which of the following is the principal mechanism of action of ethosuximide?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium channel blockade"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in the frequency of the chloride channel opening"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in GABA"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased potassium channel permeability"},{"label":"E","text":"Sodium channel blockade"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1c5bc663647","stem":"Promethazine, a phenothiazine derivative with substantial antiemetic, antitussive, and H1 histamine receptor blocking activity, has a clinical profile quite similar to diphenhydramine. Recently the FDA mandated a “black box warning” for this widely used drug. The FDA now warns against use of the drug, in all doses and forms, for children aged 2 years or younger. Fatalities have occurred in these young patients, even in response to dosages that pre- viously were considered therapeutic and safe. Which of the following is the most likely cause of death from promethazine in these patients?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete heart block followed by asystole"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertensive crisis, intracranial hemorrhage"},{"label":"C","text":"Parkinsonian-like dyskinesias, including tardive dyskinesias"},{"label":"D","text":"Severe and refractory diarrhea leading to fluid and electrolyte loss"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventilatory depression, apnea, excessive CNS depression"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9d16877bfb95","stem":"cm laceration. To reduce discomfort we first infiltrate the surrounding area with lidocaine. Which of the following functions or sensations is most likely to disappear first as the drug’s effects build up, and the last to reap- pear as the drug’s effects wear off?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autonomic efferent function"},{"label":"B","text":"Motor nerve activity"},{"label":"C","text":"Pain"},{"label":"D","text":"Pressure (deep or heavy pressure)"},{"label":"E","text":"Temperature The Central Nervous System 123"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-36c4ee685be6","stem":"A 55-year-old woman undergoes surgery. She receives several drugs for preanesthesia care, intubation, and intraoperative skeletal muscle paralysis; and a mixture of inhaled anesthetics to complete the balanced anesthesia. Toward the end of the procedure she develops hyperthermia, hypertension, hyperkalemia, tachycardia, muscle rigidity, and metabolic acidosis. Which of the following drugs is most likely to have participated in this reaction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fentanyl"},{"label":"B","text":"Halothane"},{"label":"C","text":"Ketamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Midazolam"},{"label":"E","text":"Propofol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f60651ba502","stem":"A 30-year-old woman with partial seizures is treated with vigabatrin. Which of the following is the principal mechanism of action of vigabatrin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium channel blockade"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in frequency of chloride channel opening"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in GABA"},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium channel blockade"},{"label":"E","text":"Increased potassium channel permeability"},{"label":"F","text":"NMDA receptor blockade"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5306249543e3","stem":"A patient with epilepsy is started on oral therapy with an appropriate anticonvulsant. Not long after treatment starts he manifests psychotic behav- iors that were not present before antiepileptic drug therapy started. Of the following antiepileptic agents, which is associated with the highest risk of causing psychosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antiepileptic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethosuximide"},{"label":"B","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"D","text":"Valproic acid"},{"label":"E","text":"Vigabatrin 124 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1ba86f395920","stem":"A 24-year-old woman has a history of epilepsy that is being treated with phenytoin. She is healthy otherwise. She becomes pregnant. Which of the following should we do throughout the remainder of her pregnancy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Add valproic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Discontinue all anticonvulsant medication"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase daily dietary iron intake"},{"label":"D","text":"Prescribe daily folic acid supplements"},{"label":"E","text":"Switch from the phenytoin to phenobarbital"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a76ec7c2bba5","stem":"A patient is transported to the emergency department by ambulance after repeated episodes of fainting. The cause was attributed to severe drug- induced orthostatic hypotension due to α-adrenergic blockade from one of the drug’s main side effects. Which of the following drugs was the most likely cause of this problem?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Buspirone"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpromazine"},{"label":"C","text":"Diphenhydramine"},{"label":"D","text":"Haloperidol"},{"label":"E","text":"Zolpidem"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b4da4b8a538e","stem":"A patient who is going to have an uncomfortable endoscopic procedure is pretreated with midazolam and fentanyl, both given IV. Both drugs cause generalized CNS depression and desired sedation. Which of the following is the main effect for which the midazolam was used as a supplement?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes amnesia of short duration"},{"label":"B","text":"Causes analgesia to supplement the sedative effects of fentanyl"},{"label":"C","text":"Counteracts the tachycardic effects of the fentanyl"},{"label":"D","text":"Prevents fentanyl-induced rises of blood pressure"},{"label":"E","text":"Prevents/blocks fentanyl-induced ventilatory depression The Central Nervous System 125"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8e78fb6d6906","stem":"Clozapine, as an example of the “atypical antipsychotics,” seldom is used as first-line (initial) therapy of schizophrenia. Compared with the older antipsychotics, it is associated with a much higher risk of a serious adverse response. Which of the following best summarizes what that greater risk is?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agranulocytosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Extrapyramidal side effects (parkinsonian)"},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoglycemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypotension, severe"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventilatory depression or arrest"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Psychiatry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6a4312edeec8","stem":"A woman has been taking a prescribed drug throughout her preg- nancy. Starting about a month before her expected delivery date we start administering oral vitamin K supplements, and when the baby is born he/she is given an injection of vitamin K. The goal is to reduce the risks of excessive or abnormal bleeding, caused by drug-induced impairments of hepatic vita- min K-dependent clotting factors, in the newborn. For which of the follow- ing drugs are these precautions most likely to be needed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupropion"},{"label":"B","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Methadone"},{"label":"D","text":"Naloxone"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88c3a97d1acf","stem":"A patient has frequent facial tics and spontaneous outbursts of foul language (coprolalia). Which of the following drugs would most likely be the best and probably most efficacious and safe initial treatment for this, assuming no specific contraindications to using it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clozapine"},{"label":"B","text":"Haloperidol"},{"label":"C","text":"Levodopa"},{"label":"D","text":"Methylphenidate"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenobarbital 126 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-120e12de01f4","stem":"When we administer carbidopa along with levodopa for Parkinson’s disease, we increase the bioavailability of levodopa by inhibiting the for- mation of dopamine in the gut. However, the carbidopa-induced inhibition of dopa decarboxylase favors the peripheral metabolism of levodopa to another metabolite (3-O-methyldopa) that competes with levodopa for transport across the blood-brain barrier. This is catalyzed by catechol-O- methyltransferase (COMT). Which of the following drugs inhibits COMT, and so can increase the central bioavailability and effects of levodopa?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Donepezil"},{"label":"B","text":"Entacapone"},{"label":"C","text":"Selegiline"},{"label":"D","text":"Tacrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Trihexyphenidyl"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7ba083b2fd4d","stem":"A patient has a long history of excessive alcohol consumption. He was arrested for drunk driving and was referred to a physician for therapy. The MD prescribed a drug to stifle further alcohol ingestion, to be used along with other interventions. The patient was instructed not to consume any alcohol, not to use alcohol-containing mouthwashes, or even apply alcohol-based toi- letries, because alcohol may cause a disturbing, if not dangerous interaction with his medication. The patient ignored the advice and decided to have a cocktail. Within minutes he develops flushing, a throbbing headache, nausea, and vomiting. Which of the following medications was he most likely taking?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naltrexone"},{"label":"B","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"C","text":"Disulfiram"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"E","text":"Tranylcypromine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-49a270f9da15","stem":"We start a patient with endogenous depression on a drug that selec- tively inhibits neuronal serotonin (5-HT) reuptake and has minimal effect on the reuptake of norepinephrine or dopamine. Which of the following drugs best fits this description?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amitriptyline"},{"label":"B","text":"Bupropion"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoxetine"},{"label":"D","text":"Imipramine"},{"label":"E","text":"Venlafaxine The Central Nervous System 127"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d21524a9467b","stem":"A 29-year-old man uses secobarbital and alcohol to satisfy his addic- tion to barbiturates and other CNS depressants. During the past week he has been incarcerated and is not able to obtain the drugs. He is brought to the medical ward because of the onset of severe anxiety, increased sensitiv- ity to light, dizziness, and generalized tremors due to drug withdrawal. On physical examination, he is hyperreflexic. Which of the following agents is the best choice to diminish his withdrawal symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Buspirone"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloral hydrate"},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorpromazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Lorazepam"},{"label":"E","text":"Trazodone"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b13a759e570e","stem":"A 50-year-old man has been consuming large amounts of ethanol on an almost daily basis for many years. One day, unable to find any ethanol, he ingests a large amount of methanol (wood alcohol) that he had bought for his camp lantern. Which of the following drugs should we administer to best treat the poisoning and the signs and symptoms that occurred?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aa2a766123c3","stem":"Many news reports in the spring and summer of 2006 have told of a large number of deaths of opioid abusers who had purchased and self- administered illicit drugs that contained lethal amounts of fentanyl. One patient who received this fentanyl-laced drug presents in your emergency department, barely alive. Which of the following drugs should you admin- ister first, with the best hope that it can promptly reverse the lethal effects of the fentanyl?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"B","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"C","text":"Naloxone"},{"label":"D","text":"Naltrexone"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenytoin 128 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c8d49c39cb3c","stem":"A 10-year-old boy has nocturnal enuresis. His parents take him to a clinic that specializes in management of this condition. The physician writes an order for a low dose of imipramine. After a couple of weeks on the drug, the episodes of bed-wetting decrease dramatically. Which of the following best accounts for the beneficial effects of the drug in this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alleviates depression signs and symptoms by increasing neuronal catecholamine reuptake"},{"label":"B","text":"Blocks muscarinic receptors in the bladder musculature"},{"label":"C","text":"Causes sedation such that the boy sleeps through the night without voiding"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduces renal blood flow, glomerular filtration, and urine output"},{"label":"E","text":"Releases antidiuretic hormone (ADH)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-09ab84287253","stem":"A patient is transported to your emergency department because of a seizure. A review of his history reveals that he has been treated by different physicians for different medical conditions, and there has been no dialog between the two doctors in terms of what they’ve prescribed. One physi- cian has prescribed a drug for short-term management of depression. Another has prescribed the very same drug to help the patient quit smok- ing cigarettes. Which of the following was most likely prescribed by both doctors, and was the most likely cause of the seizures?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupropion"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlordiazepoxide"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoxetine"},{"label":"D","text":"Imipramine"},{"label":"E","text":"Lithium The Central Nervous System 129"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-5e9bcea5ed74","stem":"About one year ago you diagnosed schizophrenia signs and symptoms in a 23-year-old otherwise healthy man. As a result of intensive psychother- apy, careful titration of chlorpromazine dosages, and remarkably good com- pliance on the patient’s part, he is well enough to return to work. Several months later, at a scheduled visit, you observe numerous signs and symptoms of drug-induced parkinsonism, and the patient reports rather distressing symptoms of akathisias. However, he states no recurrences of schizophrenia manifestations. Which of the following approaches is most likely to alleviate the motor and subjective parkinsonian responses, and poses the lowest risk of causing schizophrenia signs and symptoms to reappear?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Add a catechol-O-methyltransferase inhibitor (e.g., tolcapone)"},{"label":"B","text":"Add a centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitor (e.g., donepezil or tacrine)"},{"label":"C","text":"Add benztropine"},{"label":"D","text":"Add levodopa or levodopa plus carbidopa"},{"label":"E","text":"Switch from chlorpromazine to haloperidol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13c67ce2e1b0","stem":"Isoflurane and halothane are good examples of volatile liquid general anesthetics. Which of the following statements best compares or contrasts their actions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halothane and isoflurane typically raise blood pressure, via direct vasoconstrictor effects"},{"label":"B","text":"Halothane is associated with a higher risk of renal and hepatic toxicity, espe- cially in patients who have been anesthetized with this drug before"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoflurane prolongs the QT interval (ventricular repolarization), and is associ- ated with a much higher risk of causing potentially fatal ventricular tach- yarrhythmias, than halothane"},{"label":"D","text":"Isoflurane sensitizes the myocardium to the arrhythmogenic effects of cate- cholamines much more than halothane does"},{"label":"E","text":"Use of isoflurane for balanced anesthesia requires adjunctive use of nitrous oxide and neuromuscular blockers, use of halothane does not 130 Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-79a6597b6a7d","stem":"We have a patient with Parkinson’s disease, and administer a drug that acts in the CNS as an agonist for dopamine receptors. It has no direct effects on dopamine synthesis, neuronal reuptake, or metabolic inactiva- tion. Which of the following drugs best fits this description?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amantadine"},{"label":"B","text":"Apomorphine"},{"label":"C","text":"Belladonna"},{"label":"D","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"E","text":"Selegiline"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40bd2dcded91","stem":"A patient develops profound fever, skeletal muscle rigidity, and auto- nomic and systemic electrolyte imbalances as part of a severe adverse response to a psychoactive drug. The working diagnosis is neuroleptic malignant syn- drome. In addition to administering dantrolene in an attempt to restore some semblance of normal skeletal muscle function, which of the following other drugs is most likely to be given to help provide additional symptom relief?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benztropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"C","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"D","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"E","text":"Naloxone"},{"label":"F","text":"Propranolol The Central Nervous System Answers"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-0956d33bafd7","stem":"List of Abbreviations and Acronyms . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . xxiii","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c087026da28c","stem":"answer, and understood what was going on, or just made a lucky guess?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b1f1e3afda5","stem":"when you hit the wards in a very short time. You should be able to do the for","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27ca882bf90d","stem":"“duh-oh, ok.” But why?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answer